window.AI_CURRICULUM_10 = [{"id":"aiprojectcycleethics10","index":0,"name":"Revisiting AI Project Cycle & Ethical Frameworks","subtitle":"Artificial Intelligence — Unit 1","emoji":"⚖️","palette":{"from":"#38bdf8","to":"#0c4a6e"},"lessons":[{"id":"ec1","title":"The AI Project Cycle: Five Stages Revisited","icon":"🔄","kind":"lesson","story":"You teleport back into Command Deck Atlas and find the great Project Cycle map torn apart by a data storm. Before you can build any AI hero, you must piece its five stages back together in the right order.","teach":[{"title":"It's a Cycle, Not a Line","body":"The AI Project Cycle is drawn as a loop, not a straight line, because you can jump back to an earlier stage if something goes wrong later. For example, a bad Evaluation result might send you all the way back to Data Acquisition.","visual":"🔄"},{"title":"The Five Stages at a Glance","body":"In order, the stages are: Problem Scoping (define the problem), Data Acquisition (collect data), Data Exploration (study the data), Modelling (build the AI), and Evaluation (test if it works well).","visual":"🗺️"},{"title":"Deeper This Year","body":"In Class 9 you met these stages briefly. In Class 10 you go deeper into each one — especially how Problem Scoping is structured and how Modelling and Evaluation actually work.","visual":"🔍"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many stages make up the AI Project Cycle?","answer":5,"explain":"The five stages are Problem Scoping, Data Acquisition, Data Exploration, Modelling, and Evaluation."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the FIRST stage of the AI Project Cycle?","options":["Problem Scoping","Data Acquisition","Modelling","Evaluation"],"answer":0,"explain":"You must clearly define the problem in Problem Scoping before collecting any data."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which stage involves testing how well a model performs before it can be trusted for real use?","options":["Evaluation","Data Exploration","Problem Scoping","Data Acquisition"],"answer":0,"explain":"Evaluation checks a model's accuracy and reliability before it is used in the real world."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is the AI Project Cycle called a cycle rather than a straight line?","options":["Because you can loop back to an earlier stage if something isn't working","Because it repeats the exact same stage forever","Because AI projects never actually finish","Because the stages have no particular order"],"answer":0,"explain":"Poor results at any stage — like a badly trained model — can send you back to fix an earlier stage."}]},{"id":"ec2","title":"Problem Scoping: The 4Ws Deep Dive","icon":"🧭","kind":"lesson","story":"A quest board floats before you with four glowing runes: Who, What, Where, Why. The Atlas guide explains that no AI hero may be summoned until these four questions are answered clearly.","teach":[{"title":"The 4Ws Framework","body":"Problem Scoping uses four questions to pin down a problem: Who is affected (stakeholders), What is the problem, Where does it happen (context/location), and Why should it be solved (the benefit).","visual":"🧭"},{"title":"Who — the Stakeholders","body":"Stakeholders are the people directly affected by the problem or its solution, such as students, patients, farmers, or drivers. Knowing who they are keeps the AI project focused on real needs.","visual":"👥"},{"title":"Turning It Into a Problem Statement","body":"Once the four Ws are answered, they are combined into a single clear Problem Statement — a short sentence that guides every later stage of the project.","visual":"📝"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the 4Ws of Problem Scoping, 'Who' refers to?","options":["The stakeholders affected by the problem","The location where the problem occurs","The reason the problem needs solving","The dataset used to train the model"],"answer":0,"explain":"'Who' identifies the stakeholders — the people the problem affects."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"'Where' in the 4Ws helps identify?","options":["The context or location in which the problem occurs","The people affected by the problem","The benefit of solving the problem","The type of AI model to use"],"answer":0,"explain":"'Where' describes the setting or situation surrounding the problem."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"'Why' in the 4Ws mainly explains?","options":["The benefits of solving the problem for the stakeholders","The exact algorithm to be used","The names of the stakeholders","The place where data will be stored"],"answer":0,"explain":"'Why' captures the value or benefit that solving the problem will bring."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A school wants an AI that detects when students look sleepy during online classes. Who are the main stakeholders?","options":["Students and teachers","Only the app developers","The internet service provider","No one — AI projects don't need stakeholders"],"answer":0,"explain":"Students and teachers are directly affected by this problem, making them the key stakeholders."}]},{"id":"ec3","title":"Data Acquisition & Data Exploration","icon":"📡","kind":"lesson","story":"The path leads to the Data Caverns, glittering with raw data crystals of every shape. Before any can be used, you must gather them responsibly and map out what they actually show.","teach":[{"title":"Data Acquisition: Gathering Fuel for AI","body":"Data Acquisition means collecting data from reliable sources — surveys, sensors, cameras, open government portals, or existing databases — so the AI has real information to learn from.","visual":"📡"},{"title":"Reliable and Relevant, Not Just Lots","body":"More data isn't automatically better — it must also be accurate and relevant to the problem. Irrelevant or poor-quality data can teach the model the wrong lessons.","visual":"✅"},{"title":"Data Exploration: Making Sense of It","body":"Data Exploration uses charts, graphs, and summaries to study the collected data and spot patterns or trends before building any model. It helps you understand what the data is really telling you.","visual":"📊"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is a common source used during Data Acquisition?","options":["Surveys and sensors","Random guessing","The AI's imagination","A dice roll"],"answer":0,"explain":"Surveys, sensors, cameras, and open databases are all genuine sources of real-world data."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why must acquired data be relevant, not just large in quantity?","options":["Irrelevant data can mislead the model and cause poor predictions","Large datasets are always illegal to use","Relevant data is always smaller in size","It has no effect on the final model"],"answer":0,"explain":"Data that doesn't relate to the actual problem can confuse the model instead of helping it."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the main purpose of Data Exploration?","options":["To visualize and understand patterns in the data before modelling","To permanently delete unwanted data","To write the final problem statement","To test the finished model's accuracy"],"answer":0,"explain":"Data Exploration helps you understand the data's patterns before you try to build a model from it."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which tool is most commonly used during Data Exploration?","options":["Graphs and charts","Ethical framework checklists","The 4Ws template","A boss battle"],"answer":0,"explain":"Graphs and charts make it easy to spot trends and patterns hidden inside raw data."}]},{"id":"ec4","title":"Modelling & Evaluation","icon":"⚙️","kind":"lesson","story":"At the Forge of Models, two paths appear — one for models built on fixed rules, one for models that learn from experience. Whichever you choose, the forged AI must pass the Evaluation trial before it's trusted.","teach":[{"title":"Two Types of Models","body":"A rule-based model follows fixed rules written by humans and does not change on its own. A learning-based model instead learns patterns directly from data through training.","visual":"⚙️"},{"title":"Learning-Based Approaches","body":"Learning-based models are commonly built using supervised learning (learns from labelled examples), unsupervised learning (finds patterns without labels), or reinforcement learning (learns by trial, error, and reward).","visual":"🧠"},{"title":"Evaluation: Trust but Verify","body":"In Evaluation, the model is tested on data it has never seen before (testing data) to check its accuracy and reliability before it is trusted for real-world use.","visual":"🧪"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A rule-based AI model works by...","options":["Following fixed, pre-defined rules set by humans","Learning patterns automatically from data","Guessing randomly every time","Copying another AI's decisions"],"answer":0,"explain":"Rule-based models rely entirely on rules that humans define in advance, not on learning from data."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A learning-based AI model improves mainly by...","options":["Learning patterns from data through training","Following a fixed rulebook only","Being rewritten by hand every day","Ignoring all data it receives"],"answer":0,"explain":"Learning-based models are trained on data so they can find patterns on their own."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why do we test a model on data it has NOT seen before (testing data)?","options":["To check if it truly works well on new, real-world situations","To make the training process shorter","Because training data always runs out","To confuse the model on purpose"],"answer":0,"explain":"Testing on unseen data shows whether the model can generalize, not just memorize."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"When does the Evaluation stage happen in the AI Project Cycle?","options":["After Modelling, before the AI system is trusted for real use","Before Problem Scoping begins","Only after the AI has already been deployed everywhere","It never happens — models are trusted automatically"],"answer":0,"explain":"Evaluation comes right after Modelling to confirm the model is reliable enough to be used."}]},{"id":"ec5","title":"The Three Domains of AI","icon":"🌐","kind":"lesson","story":"Three glowing towers rise ahead: one hums with numbers, one watches with glass eyes, and one whispers in human words. These are the three great domains of AI, and each guards its own kind of power.","teach":[{"title":"Data Sciences","body":"Data Sciences finds patterns in structured data to make predictions, such as recommendation systems, sales forecasting, and weather prediction.","visual":"📈"},{"title":"Computer Vision","body":"Computer Vision lets machines 'see' and interpret images or video, powering things like face-unlock, self-driving cars, and medical scan analysis.","visual":"👁️"},{"title":"Natural Language Processing (NLP)","body":"NLP lets machines understand and generate human language, powering chatbots, translation tools, and voice assistants.","visual":"💬"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which AI domain helps a self-driving car recognize a pedestrian crossing the road?","options":["Computer Vision","Data Sciences","Natural Language Processing","Bioethics"],"answer":0,"explain":"Recognizing objects and people in images or video is the job of Computer Vision."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which domain would mainly power a chatbot that answers written questions in English?","options":["Natural Language Processing","Computer Vision","Data Sciences","Rule-Based Modelling"],"answer":0,"explain":"Understanding and responding to human language is the core job of NLP."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A system that predicts next month's sales using past sales numbers belongs mainly to which domain?","options":["Data Sciences","Computer Vision","Natural Language Processing","Bioethics"],"answer":0,"explain":"Finding patterns in numerical data to predict future trends is a classic Data Sciences task."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Voice assistants understanding spoken commands rely mainly on...","options":["Natural Language Processing","Computer Vision","Data Sciences","Evaluation metrics"],"answer":0,"explain":"Understanding spoken or written human language falls under Natural Language Processing."}]},{"id":"ec6","title":"What Is an Ethical Framework?","icon":"⚖️","kind":"lesson","story":"At the gates of the Ethics Sanctum, a guardian blocks your path and asks: 'Before your AI acts, can you tell me what is right?' You realize no AI hero may pass without understanding ethical frameworks.","teach":[{"title":"What Is an Ethical Framework?","body":"An ethical framework is a structured set of guidelines or principles that helps people decide what is right or wrong in a given situation.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Why AI Needs Ethics","body":"AI systems make decisions that affect real people's jobs, health, safety, and privacy. Without an ethical framework, an AI could cause serious harm even while working exactly as it was technically designed to.","visual":"🚨"},{"title":"Ethics Is Not the Same as Legal","body":"Something can be legal but still unethical, or ethical but not yet covered by law. Ethical frameworks push us to think beyond just 'is this allowed' to 'is this right.'","visual":"🤔"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"An ethical framework is best described as...","options":["A structured set of guidelines that helps decide right from wrong","A type of computer hardware","A programming language used to build AI","A dataset of labelled images"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ethical frameworks are guiding principles for moral decision-making, not technology itself."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does AI specifically need ethical frameworks?","options":["Because AI decisions can significantly affect real people's lives","Because AI cannot technically run without them","Because it makes the AI's code run faster","Because ethical frameworks are a type of algorithm"],"answer":0,"explain":"AI is now used in areas like healthcare, hiring, and safety, where mistakes seriously affect people."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which statement about ethics and legality is most accurate?","options":["Something can be legal but still unethical","Anything legal is automatically ethical","Ethics and law always mean exactly the same thing","Ethics only applies to illegal actions"],"answer":0,"explain":"Legality and ethics are related but not identical — a law can permit something many would still see as wrong."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is an example of an AI ethical concern?","options":["A hiring AI unfairly rejecting candidates based on gender","A hiring AI taking a few extra seconds to load","A hiring AI using a blue-colored interface","A hiring AI printing job listings on paper"],"answer":0,"explain":"Unfair treatment based on gender is a bias and fairness issue — a core ethical concern in AI."}]},{"id":"ec7","title":"Types of Ethical Frameworks","icon":"📜","kind":"lesson","story":"Three ethical guardians step forward, each reasoning differently about the same runaway-cart dilemma. To pass their trial, you must recognize which way of thinking each one represents.","teach":[{"title":"Consequence-Based Thinking (Utilitarian)","body":"This way of thinking judges an action by its outcomes — the best choice is whichever creates the greatest good, or the least harm, for the greatest number of people.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Duty-Based Thinking (Deontological)","body":"This way of thinking judges an action by whether it follows a fixed moral rule or duty — such as 'never lie' or 'always get consent' — regardless of what the outcome turns out to be.","visual":"📜"},{"title":"Fairness-Based Thinking","body":"This way of thinking focuses on treating every person or group equally, checking that an AI system does not discriminate or show bias against anyone.","visual":"🤝"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A utilitarian (consequence-based) approach to AI ethics chooses the action that...","options":["Creates the greatest good, or least harm, for the greatest number of people","Follows a strict rule no matter the outcome","Only benefits the company that built the AI","Is the fastest one to compute"],"answer":0,"explain":"Utilitarian thinking weighs outcomes and picks the choice with the best overall result."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A duty-based (deontological) approach to AI ethics focuses mainly on...","options":["Following moral rules or duties, regardless of the outcome","Only the final result of the action","Maximizing company profit","Choosing randomly between options"],"answer":0,"explain":"Deontological thinking says some rules — like honesty or consent — must be followed even if the outcome is imperfect."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A fairness-based ethical framework for AI mainly checks whether...","options":["The AI treats all groups of people equally, without bias","The AI runs fast enough for daily use","The AI uses the newest possible algorithm","The AI has an attractive user interface"],"answer":0,"explain":"Fairness-based thinking is about equal, unbiased treatment across different groups of people."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why are there multiple TYPES of ethical frameworks instead of just one?","options":["Because different situations may call for different ways of reasoning about right and wrong","Because AI needs more lines of code to run","Because only one type is legally required","Because computers cannot agree on anything"],"answer":0,"explain":"No single framework fits every dilemma perfectly, so different ways of reasoning are useful in different situations."}]},{"id":"ec-boss","title":"Bioethics: The Healthcare AI Trial","icon":"🏥","kind":"boss","story":"In the final ward of the Atlas, Dr. Bioethica guards the Healthcare AI wing. To earn passage, you must prove you understand bioethics — the real-world ethical framework used whenever AI meets medicine — and everything you've learned along the way.","teach":[{"title":"What Is Bioethics?","body":"Bioethics is a branch of ethics that deals with moral issues arising in medicine and healthcare. It is one of the most widely used real-world ethical frameworks, and it is increasingly applied to healthcare AI systems.","visual":"🏥"},{"title":"The Four Bioethics Principles","body":"Bioethics is commonly built on four principles: Autonomy (respecting a patient's right to decide), Beneficence (doing good), Non-maleficence (doing no harm), and Justice (fair treatment and access for everyone).","visual":"🧬"},{"title":"Case Study: A Biased Diagnosis Tool","body":"An AI tool designed to scan skin images for signs of disease was trained mostly on images of light skin tones. It ended up performing less accurately for patients with darker skin — a bioethics review flagged this as a violation of Justice, and the tool was sent back for retraining with fairer, more diverse data.","visual":"🔬"}],"boss":{"name":"Dr. Bioethica, Guardian of the Healthcare Ward","taunt":"Diagnose my logic before I diagnose your grade!"},"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Bioethics is an ethical framework most closely associated with which field?","options":["Healthcare and medicine","Computer hardware design","Professional sports","Data storage"],"answer":0,"explain":"Bioethics deals specifically with moral issues that arise in medicine and healthcare."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The bioethics principle 'Autonomy' means...","options":["Respecting a patient's right to make their own informed decisions","Always doing whatever the doctor decides alone","Ignoring patient consent to save time","Maximizing hospital profit above all else"],"answer":0,"explain":"Autonomy protects a patient's right to choose what happens to their own body and care."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The bioethics principle 'Justice' applied to a healthcare AI mainly means...","options":["Making sure the AI treats and benefits all patient groups fairly","Punishing an AI whenever it makes a mistake","Following every local law exactly","Making the AI's source code publicly available"],"answer":0,"explain":"Justice in bioethics is about fair, equal treatment and access across all groups of people."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the case study, why was the skin-scan AI flagged as an ethical problem?","options":["It performed less accurately for patients with darker skin due to biased training data","It was too slow to load results","It cost too much money to build","It used too much electricity"],"answer":0,"explain":"The tool's training data lacked diversity, causing unfair, less accurate results for some patients."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which AI Project Cycle stage would you revisit FIRST to fix a biased, unrepresentative dataset?","options":["Data Acquisition","Problem Scoping","Evaluation","Deployment"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since the bias came from the data itself, you would go back to Data Acquisition to collect fairer, more diverse data."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which AI domain would a healthcare AI that analyzes skin images or X-rays belong to?","options":["Computer Vision","Natural Language Processing","Data Sciences only","None of these"],"answer":0,"explain":"Interpreting images like skin scans or X-rays is a Computer Vision task."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Judging the skin-scan AI by 'did it cause less overall harm and more overall good' uses which type of ethical reasoning?","options":["Consequence-based (utilitarian) reasoning","Duty-based reasoning only","No ethical reasoning at all","Legal reasoning only"],"answer":0,"explain":"Weighing overall harm versus good is the hallmark of consequence-based (utilitarian) thinking."}]}]},{"id":"advancedmodeling10","index":1,"name":"Advanced Concepts of Modeling in AI","subtitle":"Artificial Intelligence — Unit 2","emoji":"🧠","theme":"Neon control-room, glowing circuits, holographic brains","palette":{"from":"#fb7185","to":"#881337","accent":"#4c0519"},"lessons":[{"id":"am1","title":"Three Circles of Power","icon":"🎯","kind":"lesson","story":"You step into the Modeling Wing of the Study Atlas and find three glowing rings nested inside each other, humming with data. A hologram whispers: 'Name us correctly, or the door stays sealed.'","teach":[{"title":"Artificial Intelligence (AI)","body":"AI is the biggest circle — the broad science of making machines act smart, mimicking human abilities like thinking, understanding language, or recognising images. Anything a machine does that looks 'intelligent' falls under AI.","visual":"🤖"},{"title":"Machine Learning (ML)","body":"ML is a smaller circle inside AI. Instead of a programmer writing every rule by hand, an ML system learns patterns from data and improves with experience, like a student learning from many solved examples.","visual":"📊"},{"title":"Deep Learning (DL)","body":"DL is the smallest circle, inside ML. It uses layered structures called neural networks (inspired loosely by the brain) to learn very complex patterns, such as recognising faces in photos or understanding speech.","visual":"🧬"},{"title":"The nested relationship","body":"Picture three rings: AI is the outer ring, ML sits inside AI, and DL sits inside ML. Every DL system is ML, and every ML system is AI — but not every AI system uses ML, and not every ML system uses DL.","visual":"⭕"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which statement best describes how AI, ML and DL relate to each other?","options":["DL is a part of ML, and ML is a part of AI, like nested circles","AI is a part of ML, and ML is a part of DL","AI, ML and DL are three completely unrelated fields","DL is the biggest field, containing both AI and ML"],"answer":0,"explain":"AI is the broadest field; ML is a subset of AI; DL is a further subset of ML, using layered neural networks."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the key idea behind Machine Learning, compared to traditional programming?","options":["The system learns patterns from data instead of following only hand-written rules","The system can only follow instructions typed by a programmer","The system has no way to improve its performance","The system never uses any data at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"ML systems find patterns in data and improve with experience, rather than relying purely on explicitly coded rules."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is a Deep Learning system most likely to be used for?","options":["Recognising faces in thousands of photos using a many-layered neural network","Adding two numbers using a fixed formula","Sorting a list of names alphabetically","Checking if a light switch is on or off"],"answer":0,"explain":"Deep Learning shines at complex pattern recognition tasks like image or face recognition, using layered neural networks."}]},{"id":"am2","title":"The Data Vault","icon":"🗄️","kind":"lesson","story":"A vault door in the Modeling Wing is covered with labelled drawers — 'Training', 'Testing', 'Features', 'Labels'. The vault keeper says every AI model is only as good as what's stored inside these drawers.","teach":[{"title":"Data, the raw fuel","body":"Data is any collected fact — numbers, text, images or sounds — that a model can learn from. Good, relevant data is essential; a model trained on poor data will make poor decisions.","visual":"📦"},{"title":"Features and labels","body":"A feature is an individual measurable property used as input, like a fruit's colour or weight. A label is the answer or category we want the model to predict, like 'mango' or 'apple'.","visual":"🏷️"},{"title":"Training data vs testing data","body":"Training data is the portion of data shown to the model so it can learn patterns. Testing data is a separate, unseen portion used afterwards to check how well the model actually learned.","visual":"🎓"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a model that predicts fruit type from colour and weight, what are 'colour' and 'weight' called?","options":["Features","Labels","Testing data","Neural networks"],"answer":0,"explain":"Features are the measurable input properties fed into the model, such as colour and weight."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why do we keep a separate set of testing data instead of testing on the training data itself?","options":["To honestly check how well the model performs on data it has not already seen","Because training data is not allowed to be used at all","Because testing data is always larger than training data","To make the model forget everything it learned"],"answer":0,"explain":"Testing on unseen data gives a fair check of whether the model has really learned useful patterns, not just memorised examples."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is a 'label' in a dataset used to identify fruits?","options":["The correct answer/category the model should predict, such as 'mango'","The colour of the fruit only","The total number of fruits in the dataset","The programming language used to build the model"],"answer":0,"explain":"The label is the target output the model is trying to learn to predict correctly."}]},{"id":"am3","title":"Two Paths of the Machine","icon":"🛤️","kind":"lesson","story":"Two glowing paths fork ahead of you: one lined with rigid stone rulebooks, the other flowing with shifting data streams. A guardian asks which path builds smarter machines — and why.","teach":[{"title":"Rule-based approach","body":"In a rule-based model, a human expert writes explicit 'if-then' rules that the machine follows exactly, like 'if temperature > 40°C, then say it is hot'. It does not learn from data; its knowledge comes only from the rules given.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Learning-based approach","body":"In a learning-based model, the machine is given lots of examples (data) and figures out the patterns and rules itself, improving as it sees more data — much like ML systems work.","visual":"🌱"},{"title":"Comparing the two","body":"Rule-based systems are predictable but cannot handle situations the rules did not anticipate. Learning-based systems can adapt to new patterns in data, but need good quality data to learn well.","visual":"⚖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a rule-based AI model, where does the machine's knowledge come from?","options":["Explicit if-then rules written by humans","Patterns the machine discovers on its own from data","Random guessing","Copying answers from another machine"],"answer":0,"explain":"Rule-based models rely entirely on rules explicitly coded by humans, not on learning from data."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is a key limitation of a rule-based approach?","options":["It cannot handle situations that were not covered by its predefined rules","It always learns new rules automatically from data","It requires no human effort to build","It always performs better than learning-based models"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since rule-based systems only follow the rules they were given, unexpected situations outside those rules can trip them up."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A spam filter that studies thousands of past emails to learn what spam looks like is using which approach?","options":["Learning-based approach","Rule-based approach only","Neither approach","It is not an AI model at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"The filter learns patterns from example data (past emails), which is the defining trait of a learning-based approach."}]},{"id":"am4","title":"The Labelled Library","icon":"📚","kind":"lesson","story":"In a glowing library, every book already has a summary card attached telling you exactly what it's about. An AI librarian explains: 'This is how I learn best — when someone already tells me the right answers.'","teach":[{"title":"Supervised learning","body":"In supervised learning, the model is trained on labelled data — data where the correct answer is already known. The model learns the relationship between the input and the correct output, then predicts labels for new, unseen inputs.","visual":"✅"},{"title":"Real example: Teachable Machine","body":"Tools like Google's Teachable Machine let you show the model many labelled photos, for example 'cat' or 'dog', so it learns to correctly label a brand-new photo based on what it saw during training.","visual":"📸"},{"title":"Why it needs labels","body":"Just like a student who checks their answers against a solved answer key, a supervised model needs many correctly labelled examples to know if its predictions are right or wrong, and to improve.","visual":"🔑"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What makes a learning approach 'supervised'?","options":["The training data comes with known, correct labels/answers","The model never sees any data","A human physically watches the machine every second","The model rewards and punishes itself with no data"],"answer":0,"explain":"Supervised learning uses labelled data, where each example already has the correct answer attached."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is an example of supervised learning?","options":["Training a model on photos labelled 'cat' or 'dog' so it can label new photos","Letting a robot wander randomly with no data or feedback","Grouping customers by shopping habits with no predefined categories","A thermostat that just follows a fixed temperature rule"],"answer":0,"explain":"Using labelled examples (cat/dog) to train a model to predict labels on new data is the core idea of supervised learning."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does a supervised model need labelled training data?","options":["So it can compare its predictions to the correct answers and learn from mistakes","Because labels make the data file smaller","Labels are optional and never actually used","So the model can skip training entirely"],"answer":0,"explain":"Labels act as an answer key, letting the model check and correct its predictions during training."}]},{"id":"am5","title":"The Unlabelled Cave & The Trial Chamber","icon":"🕳️","kind":"lesson","story":"Beyond the library lies a dark cave with no labels anywhere, and past that, a chamber of trial-and-error puzzles that reward and punish every move. Two very different kinds of AI minds are trained here.","teach":[{"title":"Unsupervised learning","body":"In unsupervised learning, the model is given data with NO labels. It must find hidden patterns or structure on its own, such as grouping similar items together, without ever being told the 'correct' answer.","visual":"🌀"},{"title":"Real example: the AI Drum Machine","body":"Google's AI Drum Machine experiment groups similar drum sounds together without anyone labelling them as 'kick' or 'snare' beforehand — it discovers the grouping purely from the sound data itself.","visual":"🥁"},{"title":"Reinforcement learning","body":"In reinforcement learning, an agent learns by interacting with an environment, trying actions, and receiving rewards or penalties. Over many attempts, it learns which actions lead to the best outcome — like training a dog with treats.","visual":"🎮"},{"title":"Real example: game-playing AI","body":"An AI learning to play a video game through reinforcement learning tries different moves, earns points (rewards) for good moves and loses points for bad ones, gradually learning a winning strategy.","visual":"🏆"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the key difference between supervised and unsupervised learning?","options":["Unsupervised learning uses data with no labels; supervised learning uses labelled data","Unsupervised learning always needs more labels than supervised learning","There is no difference between them","Supervised learning never uses any data"],"answer":0,"explain":"Supervised learning relies on labelled data, while unsupervised learning works with unlabelled data to find hidden structure."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How does reinforcement learning train an agent?","options":["Through trial and error, using rewards and penalties from an environment","By giving it a complete labelled answer key before it starts","By grouping unlabelled data into clusters","It requires no interaction with any environment"],"answer":0,"explain":"Reinforcement learning agents learn by acting, observing rewards or penalties, and adjusting behaviour to maximise reward."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A robot vacuum that gets a virtual 'reward' for cleaning more floor area and learns better cleaning paths over time is using which learning type?","options":["Reinforcement learning","Supervised learning","Unsupervised learning","Rule-based approach only"],"answer":0,"explain":"Learning from rewards through interaction with the environment is the defining trait of reinforcement learning."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Grouping customers into segments based on shopping patterns, with no predefined categories given in advance, is an example of which learning type?","options":["Unsupervised learning","Supervised learning","Reinforcement learning","Rule-based approach"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since there are no labels telling the model the 'correct' groups in advance, this is unsupervised learning."}]},{"id":"am6","title":"Sort or Predict?","icon":"🔀","kind":"lesson","story":"A supervised-learning gatekeeper blocks your way with two machines: one that sorts items into boxes, one that predicts exact numbers. 'Tell me which machine to use,' it demands, 'or you're stuck here forever.'","teach":[{"title":"Classification model","body":"Classification is a supervised learning task where the model predicts a category or class from a fixed set of options, such as 'spam' or 'not spam', or 'cat', 'dog', or 'rabbit'. The output is discrete (a label).","visual":"🏷️"},{"title":"Regression model","body":"Regression is a supervised learning task where the model predicts a continuous numeric value, such as predicting tomorrow's temperature or the price of a house. The output is a number, not a category.","visual":"📈"},{"title":"How to tell them apart","body":"Ask: is the answer a category/label (classification), or a number that could be anything within a range (regression)? 'Will it rain tomorrow?' is classification (yes/no); 'How many mm of rain tomorrow?' is regression.","visual":"❓"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Predicting whether an email is 'spam' or 'not spam' is an example of which task?","options":["Classification","Regression","Clustering","Association"],"answer":0,"explain":"The output is a category chosen from a fixed set (spam or not spam), which is classification."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Predicting the exact price of a house in rupees based on its size and location is an example of which task?","options":["Regression","Classification","Clustering","Reinforcement learning only"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since the output is a continuous numeric value (price), this is a regression task."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the main difference between classification and regression?","options":["Classification predicts a category/label; regression predicts a continuous number","Classification always predicts numbers; regression always predicts categories","They are exactly the same technique with different names","Regression can only be used with unlabelled data"],"answer":0,"explain":"Classification outputs discrete labels, while regression outputs continuous numeric values."}]},{"id":"am7","title":"Groups and Connections","icon":"🧩","kind":"lesson","story":"In the unsupervised wing, you find shelves of items nobody has sorted, and a wall of tangled strings connecting products bought together. Two puzzles, two skills, one goal: find the hidden structure.","teach":[{"title":"Clustering","body":"Clustering is an unsupervised learning task that groups similar data points together into clusters, without being told the group names in advance. For example, grouping customers with similar buying habits.","visual":"🔵🔵🟢"},{"title":"Association","body":"Association is an unsupervised learning task that finds interesting relationships or rules between items in large datasets, such as 'customers who buy bread often also buy butter'. It is widely used in market-basket analysis.","visual":"🍞🧈"},{"title":"Clustering vs Association","body":"Clustering groups similar items together into clusters; association finds rules describing how items relate or occur together. Both work on unlabelled data but answer different questions.","visual":"🔗"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Grouping shoppers into segments like 'budget shoppers' and 'premium shoppers' based on similarity in behaviour, with no predefined labels, is an example of:","options":["Clustering","Association","Classification","Regression"],"answer":0,"explain":"Clustering groups similar data points together without predefined category labels."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Discovering the rule 'people who buy bread often also buy butter' from shopping data is an example of:","options":["Association","Clustering","Classification","Reinforcement learning"],"answer":0,"explain":"Association mining finds relationships or rules between items that frequently occur together, as in market-basket analysis."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Both clustering and association are subcategories of which broader learning type?","options":["Unsupervised learning","Supervised learning","Reinforcement learning","Rule-based approach"],"answer":0,"explain":"Clustering and association both work on unlabelled data, making them subcategories of unsupervised learning."}]},{"id":"am8","title":"The Brain Room","icon":"🧠","kind":"lesson","story":"A glowing web of tiny lights pulses on the wall ahead, layer after layer flickering in sequence. 'Welcome to the Brain Room,' says a voice. 'Here, deep learning models come alive.'","teach":[{"title":"What is a neural network?","body":"A neural network is a model loosely inspired by the human brain. It is made of small units called neurons (or nodes), arranged in connected layers, that work together to process information and learn patterns from data.","visual":"🕸️"},{"title":"Artificial Neural Network (ANN)","body":"An ANN is the basic deep learning structure: an input layer that receives data, one or more hidden layers that process it, and an output layer that gives the result. Each connection has a 'weight' that strengthens or weakens the signal.","visual":"🔷"},{"title":"Convolutional Neural Network (CNN)","body":"A CNN is a special type of neural network designed mainly for images. It scans small patches of an image to detect features like edges, shapes, and textures, then combines them to recognise objects such as faces or animals.","visual":"🖼️"},{"title":"ANN vs CNN","body":"A general ANN can be used for many kinds of data, while a CNN is specially built to handle the grid-like structure of images efficiently, making it the go-to choice for image recognition tasks.","visual":"⚡"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is a neural network loosely inspired by?","options":["The human brain and its interconnected neurons","A dictionary of if-then rules","A single calculator function","A physical library of books"],"answer":0,"explain":"Neural networks are loosely inspired by the structure of neurons in the human brain, connected in layers."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which layers make up a basic Artificial Neural Network (ANN)?","options":["Input layer, one or more hidden layers, and an output layer","Only an input layer, nothing else","Only a database and a printer","Only labels and rules, no layers"],"answer":0,"explain":"A basic ANN structure has an input layer, hidden layer(s) for processing, and an output layer for the result."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) especially well-suited for image recognition?","options":["It scans patches of an image to detect features like edges and shapes before recognising objects","It ignores all image data completely","It only works with text, never images","It uses if-then rules instead of learning from data"],"answer":0,"explain":"CNNs are specially designed to process grid-like image data by detecting local features such as edges and textures."}]},{"id":"am9","title":"The Decision Engine","icon":"⚙️","kind":"lesson","story":"At the heart of the Brain Room sits a single glowing node, wired to dozens of blinking inputs. 'Watch closely,' the voice says. 'This is the exact moment a machine decides.'","teach":[{"title":"Inputs and weights","body":"A simple neural network decision starts with inputs (data values) entering the network. Each input is multiplied by a weight, a number showing how important that input is to the final decision — bigger weight means more influence.","visual":"➡️"},{"title":"Summing it up","body":"The neuron adds up all the weighted inputs together into a single combined value, similar to how you might tally scores from different judges, each judge's opinion counting differently based on their weight.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"The activation decision","body":"This combined value passes through an activation step that decides the neuron's output, for example 'fire' (output close to 1) if the sum crosses a threshold, or 'stay quiet' (output close to 0) if it does not.","visual":"💡"},{"title":"Putting it together","body":"Across many neurons and layers, this weigh-sum-decide process repeats, gradually combining simple decisions into a complex final decision, like recognising a picture as a 'cat' with high confidence.","visual":"🐱"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a simple neural network, what does a 'weight' represent?","options":["How important a given input is to the final decision","The exact colour of the input data","The total number of layers in the network","The programmer's name"],"answer":0,"explain":"A weight is a number that shows how strongly an input should influence the neuron's decision."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"After a neuron adds up its weighted inputs, what happens next?","options":["An activation step decides whether the neuron 'fires' and what output it produces","The network immediately forgets the result","The data is deleted permanently","Nothing happens; the process ends there"],"answer":0,"explain":"The summed value passes through an activation step, which determines the neuron's final output."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How does a neural network typically reach a complex decision, like identifying an object in a picture?","options":["By repeating the weigh-sum-decide process across many connected neurons and layers","By using a single neuron with no connections at all","By randomly guessing with no calculation","By looking up the answer in a fixed rulebook only"],"answer":0,"explain":"Complex decisions emerge from many simple weigh-sum-decide steps happening across layers of connected neurons."}]},{"id":"am-boss","title":"Boss: The Modeling Overmind","icon":"👾","kind":"boss","story":"The Modeling Overmind rises from the core of the Brain Room, built from every concept you've learned — nested circles, forked paths, labelled libraries, and glowing neurons. It challenges you to prove you truly understand modeling in AI.","boss":{"name":"The Modeling Overmind","taunt":"You think you can out-model a mind built from a thousand neurons? Prove it, learner!"},"teach":[{"title":"Quick recap","body":"AI is the broadest field; ML learns from data inside AI; DL uses neural networks inside ML. Models can be rule-based or learning-based, and learning-based models fall into supervised, unsupervised, or reinforcement learning.","visual":"🗺️"},{"title":"Remember the subcategories","body":"Supervised learning splits into classification (categories) and regression (numbers). Unsupervised learning splits into clustering (grouping) and association (relationships). Deep learning includes ANN and CNN.","visual":"🧭"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which correctly orders these fields from broadest to narrowest?","options":["AI, then ML, then DL","DL, then ML, then AI","ML, then AI, then DL","They are all the exact same size"],"answer":0,"explain":"AI is the broadest field, ML is a subset of AI, and DL is a further subset of ML."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A model that follows only human-written if-then rules, with no learning from data, uses which approach?","options":["Rule-based approach","Learning-based approach","Reinforcement learning","Deep learning"],"answer":0,"explain":"A rule-based model relies purely on explicit rules written by humans, not on learning from data."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which learning paradigm uses labelled data to train the model?","options":["Supervised learning","Unsupervised learning","Reinforcement learning only","None of them use data"],"answer":0,"explain":"Supervised learning specifically requires labelled data so the model can learn the correct input-output relationship."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Predicting a student's exact exam score (a number) from hours studied is an example of:","options":["Regression","Classification","Clustering","Association"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since the output is a continuous number (the score), this is a regression task."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which unsupervised technique would you use to find that 'people who buy pasta often also buy pasta sauce'?","options":["Association","Clustering","Classification","Regression"],"answer":0,"explain":"Association mining finds relationships between items that frequently occur together, like pasta and pasta sauce."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which type of neural network is specially designed for recognising patterns in images?","options":["Convolutional Neural Network (CNN)","A rule-based system","A spreadsheet formula","A simple if-then statement"],"answer":0,"explain":"CNNs are specially built to process grid-like image data by detecting local visual features."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a neural network, what determines how strongly one input influences the final decision?","options":["Its weight","Its colour","Its file size","Its alphabetical order"],"answer":0,"explain":"The weight assigned to each input controls how much that input influences the neuron's output."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"An AI agent that learns to play chess by receiving rewards for good moves and penalties for bad ones is using:","options":["Reinforcement learning","Supervised learning","Unsupervised learning","Rule-based approach only"],"answer":0,"explain":"Learning through rewards and penalties from interacting with an environment is reinforcement learning."}]}]},{"id":"evaluatingmodels10","index":2,"name":"Evaluating Models","subtitle":"Artificial Intelligence — Unit 3","emoji":"📏","palette":{"from":"#4ade80","to":"#14532d"},"lessons":[{"id":"em1","title":"Why We Evaluate AI Models","icon":"🔍","kind":"lesson","story":"Cadet, you've just trained your first AI model: a rookie spam detector. It looks great in the lab, but Command won't let it near real inboxes until you can PROVE how good it really is. Time to learn why evaluation is the difference between a trustworthy AI and a dangerous guess.","teach":[{"title":"What Is Model Evaluation?","body":"Model evaluation means testing how well a trained AI model performs, usually on data it has never seen before. It turns a vague feeling of 'this seems good' into real, measurable numbers.","visual":"🔍"},{"title":"Why It Matters","body":"A model that isn't properly evaluated might fail silently in the real world, wrongly diagnosing a patient, missing real spam, or making unfair decisions. Evaluation catches these problems before deployment, not after.","visual":"⚠️"},{"title":"Evaluation Guides Improvement","body":"Evaluation isn't just a final report card. It tells developers exactly where a model is weak, so they know what to fix, collect more data for, or redesign.","visual":"🛠️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does 'evaluating an AI model' mean?","options":["Testing how well the model performs on new, unseen data","Deleting the model after it is trained","Making the model's code file bigger","Writing more comments in the code"],"answer":0,"explain":"Evaluation checks a model's real performance, usually on data it hasn't seen during training."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why can't we fully trust a model just because it did well on its training data?","options":["It might have simply memorized the training examples instead of learning general patterns","Training data is always fake and made up","Models forget everything the moment training ends","Training data never contains any numbers"],"answer":0,"explain":"A model can 'memorize' training examples without truly learning to generalize to new situations."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A weather-prediction model was tested on 50 days and predicted correctly on 40 of them. How many predictions were wrong?","answer":10,"explain":"50 total predictions minus 40 correct predictions leaves 10 wrong predictions."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which is the BEST reason to evaluate a model before deploying it in a real hospital app?","options":["To check that the model won't make harmful wrong predictions on real patients","To make the training file smaller","To make the code run in a different programming language","To change the color of the app's icon"],"answer":0,"explain":"In high-stakes settings like healthcare, evaluation helps confirm the model is safe and reliable before real use."}]},{"id":"em2","title":"Train-Test Split","icon":"✂️","kind":"lesson","story":"Before your spam detector can be graded fairly, you must split your dataset in two: one pile to teach it, and a completely separate pile to test it. Mix them up, and your 'evaluation' becomes worthless. Learn the golden rule of train-test split.","teach":[{"title":"Training Set vs Test Set","body":"A dataset is usually split into a training set, which the model learns from, and a test set, which is kept aside and used only to check performance afterward. A common split is 80% training and 20% testing.","visual":"✂️"},{"title":"Why Keep Them Separate?","body":"Testing a model on data it already saw during training is like grading a student using the exact questions they memorized the answers to. It doesn't prove real understanding, so the test set must stay unseen until evaluation time.","visual":"🚫"},{"title":"A Fair Test of Real-World Performance","body":"Because the test set represents 'new' data, good performance on it is a much better sign that the model will also work well on real, future data it encounters after deployment.","visual":"🌍"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the purpose of the 'test set' in a train-test split?","options":["To check how well the model performs on data it has never seen before","To train the model faster","To store backup copies of the training data","To make the overall dataset bigger"],"answer":0,"explain":"The test set is held back from training so it can give an honest check of performance on new data."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A dataset has 1000 images. Using an 80/20 train-test split, how many images go into the TEST set?","answer":200,"explain":"20% of 1000 is 0.2 x 1000 = 200 images for testing."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A dataset of 500 records is split so that 350 records are used for training. How many records are in the test set?","answer":150,"explain":"500 total minus 350 for training leaves 150 records for testing."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why shouldn't a model be tested using the exact same data it was trained on?","options":["Because the model may just be recalling memorized answers rather than truly predicting well","Because test data is not allowed to have labels","Because training data is always stored in a different file format","Because it makes the dataset file too colorful"],"answer":0,"explain":"Reusing training data for testing can make a model look better than it actually is on new data."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A dataset of 2000 rows is split 70/30 (train/test). How many rows are used for TRAINING?","answer":1400,"explain":"70% of 2000 is 0.7 x 2000 = 1400 rows used for training."}]},{"id":"em3","title":"Accuracy and Error","icon":"🎯","kind":"lesson","story":"Your spam detector has finally faced its test set. Command wants one simple number first: how often was it right, and how often was it wrong? Time to calculate accuracy and error like a true AI cadet.","teach":[{"title":"Accuracy","body":"Accuracy is the fraction of predictions a model got correct out of all predictions it made. Accuracy = (number of correct predictions) / (total number of predictions).","visual":"🎯"},{"title":"Error Rate","body":"Error is the fraction of predictions that were wrong. Error rate = (number of wrong predictions) / (total predictions), which is the same as 1 minus the accuracy.","visual":"❌"},{"title":"Two Sides of the Same Coin","body":"Accuracy and error always add up to 1 (or 100%). A model with 90% accuracy has exactly 10% error, so improving one automatically improves the other.","visual":"🪙"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does 'accuracy' measure in an AI model?","options":["The fraction of predictions that were correct out of all predictions made","The speed at which the model makes predictions","The number of features used to build the model","The total size of the dataset used for training"],"answer":0,"explain":"Accuracy = correct predictions divided by total predictions."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A model made 80 correct predictions out of 100 total predictions. What is its accuracy as a decimal?","answer":0.8,"explain":"Accuracy = 80 / 100 = 0.8."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the same model (80 correct out of 100 total), what is its ERROR RATE as a decimal?","answer":0.2,"explain":"Error rate = 1 - accuracy = 1 - 0.8 = 0.2 (the 20 wrong predictions out of 100)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A quiz-grading AI checked 250 answers and got 225 of them right. What is its accuracy as a decimal?","answer":0.9,"explain":"Accuracy = 225 / 250 = 0.9."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the main risk of only checking a model's accuracy on its TRAINING data before release?","options":["The model may perform poorly on new, real-world data despite looking fine on paper","The model will run out of computer memory instantly","The training dataset will automatically delete itself","The accuracy score will become a negative number"],"answer":0,"explain":"Training accuracy alone can hide problems that only show up on new, unseen data — this is why a separate test set matters."}]},{"id":"em4","title":"What Is Classification?","icon":"📧","kind":"lesson","story":"Your spam detector doesn't just guess randomly, it sorts every email into one of two boxes: 'Spam' or 'Not Spam'. This is classification. But to grade it properly, you need four special labels for every possible outcome. Meet TP, FP, TN, and FN.","teach":[{"title":"What Is Classification?","body":"Classification is an AI task where the model sorts input into categories, or classes, such as 'spam' vs 'not spam', or 'cat' vs 'dog'. In the simplest case there are just two classes, called binary classification.","visual":"📧"},{"title":"Positive vs Negative Class","body":"In binary classification, one class is labeled 'Positive' (the thing you're trying to detect, like spam) and the other is 'Negative' (like a normal email). Every prediction is then either correct or wrong.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"Four Possible Outcomes","body":"True Positive (TP): correctly predicted positive. False Positive (FP): wrongly predicted positive. True Negative (TN): correctly predicted negative. False Negative (FN): wrongly predicted negative.","visual":"🧩"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a spam classifier where 'Spam' is the positive class, what is a True Positive (TP)?","options":["A spam email that the model correctly labeled as spam","A normal email correctly labeled as not spam","A normal email wrongly labeled as spam","A spam email wrongly labeled as not spam"],"answer":0,"explain":"A True Positive is when the model correctly catches an actual positive case (real spam correctly flagged)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is a False Negative (FN) in the same spam classifier?","options":["An actual spam email that the model missed and labeled as not spam","A normal email correctly labeled as not spam","A spam email correctly caught as spam","A normal email wrongly flagged as spam"],"answer":0,"explain":"A False Negative happens when the model wrongly says 'negative' for something that was actually positive — it missed real spam."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is a False Positive (FP)?","options":["A normal (non-spam) email that the model wrongly labeled as spam","A spam email that was correctly caught as spam","A normal email that was correctly passed through","A spam email that the user deleted manually"],"answer":0,"explain":"A False Positive is a false alarm: the model says 'positive' but the true answer was actually negative."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A disease-detection AI checked 20 patients. It correctly identified 6 sick patients (TP) and correctly cleared 10 healthy patients (TN). The rest of its predictions were errors (FP + FN combined). How many errors did it make?","answer":4,"explain":"20 total patients minus 6 correct positives minus 10 correct negatives leaves 20 - 6 - 10 = 4 errors."}]},{"id":"em5","title":"The Confusion Matrix","icon":"🧩","kind":"lesson","story":"Four labels are hard to juggle in your head, so Command hands you a tool: the confusion matrix, a simple grid that lays out every TP, FP, TN, and FN at a glance. Build one from your test results and calculate real accuracy from it.","teach":[{"title":"What Is a Confusion Matrix?","body":"A confusion matrix is a small table (grid) that shows the counts of TP, FP, TN, and FN together for a classifier's predictions on the test set. It lets you see everything the model got right and wrong in one place.","visual":"🧩"},{"title":"Total Predictions","body":"Adding up all four cells of the matrix gives the total number of predictions made: Total = TP + TN + FP + FN.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"Accuracy From the Matrix","body":"Once you have the matrix, accuracy is easy to compute: Accuracy = (TP + TN) / (TP + TN + FP + FN), the correct predictions divided by all predictions.","visual":"🎯"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A confusion matrix has TP=15, FP=5, FN=3, TN=27. What is the TOTAL number of predictions?","answer":50,"explain":"Total = TP + FP + FN + TN = 15 + 5 + 3 + 27 = 50."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the same matrix (TP=15, FP=5, FN=3, TN=27), what is the accuracy as a decimal?","answer":0.84,"explain":"Accuracy = (TP + TN) / Total = (15 + 27) / 50 = 42 / 50 = 0.84."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A confusion matrix shows TP=40, TN=40, FP=10, FN=10. What is the accuracy as a decimal?","answer":0.8,"explain":"Accuracy = (40 + 40) / (40 + 40 + 10 + 10) = 80 / 100 = 0.8."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a confusion matrix, which two values represent CORRECT predictions?","options":["True Positives and True Negatives","False Positives and False Negatives","True Positives and False Positives","True Negatives and False Negatives"],"answer":0,"explain":"TP and TN are both cases where the model's prediction matched the real answer — the correct ones."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A model's confusion matrix is TP=8, FP=2, FN=4, TN=6. What is the ERROR RATE as a decimal?","answer":0.3,"explain":"Total = 8+2+4+6 = 20. Error rate = (FP+FN)/Total = (2+4)/20 = 6/20 = 0.3."}]},{"id":"em6","title":"Precision, Recall and F1 Score","icon":"⚖️","kind":"lesson","story":"Accuracy alone isn't telling the full story: your spam dataset is lopsided, with far more normal mail than spam. Command teaches you three sharper tools: precision, recall, and F1 score, to judge a classifier fairly.","teach":[{"title":"Precision","body":"Precision asks: of everything the model flagged as positive, how much was actually positive? Precision = TP / (TP + FP). High precision means few false alarms.","visual":"🎯"},{"title":"Recall","body":"Recall asks: of everything that was actually positive, how much did the model catch? Recall = TP / (TP + FN). High recall means few missed cases.","visual":"🕸️"},{"title":"F1 Score","body":"F1 score combines precision and recall into a single balanced number: F1 = 2 x (Precision x Recall) / (Precision + Recall). It is useful when both false alarms and missed cases matter.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Why Not Just Use Accuracy?","body":"With imbalanced data, like 95 normal emails and only 5 spam emails, a lazy model that always predicts 'not spam' scores 95% accuracy but catches 0% of the real spam. Precision, recall and F1 reveal this weakness.","visual":"⚠️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A classifier's confusion matrix is TP=30, FP=10, FN=20, TN=40. What is the PRECISION as a decimal?","answer":0.75,"explain":"Precision = TP / (TP + FP) = 30 / (30 + 10) = 30 / 40 = 0.75."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the same matrix (TP=30, FP=10, FN=20, TN=40), what is the RECALL as a decimal?","answer":0.6,"explain":"Recall = TP / (TP + FN) = 30 / (30 + 20) = 30 / 50 = 0.6."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using precision = 0.75 and recall = 0.6 from the same matrix, what is the F1 score, rounded to 2 decimal places?","answer":0.67,"explain":"F1 = 2 x (0.75 x 0.6) / (0.75 + 0.6) = 2 x 0.45 / 1.35 = 0.9 / 1.35 = 0.6667, which rounds to 0.67."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A spam filter has very high precision but low recall. What does that mean?","options":["When it flags an email as spam it's almost always right, but it misses a lot of actual spam","It catches every single spam email but also wrongly flags many good emails","It never makes any mistakes at all, on any email","It has nothing to do with detecting spam emails"],"answer":0,"explain":"High precision means few false alarms (FP is low), while low recall means many real positives are being missed (FN is high)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is the F1 score useful when evaluating a classifier?","options":["It balances precision and recall into one score, useful when both false alarms and missed cases matter","It only measures how fast the model makes predictions","It completely replaces the need for a confusion matrix","It is always exactly equal to accuracy"],"answer":0,"explain":"F1 is the harmonic mean of precision and recall, giving one balanced score instead of looking at each separately."}]},{"id":"em7","title":"Ethics of Evaluation: Bias, Transparency, Accuracy","icon":"🧭","kind":"lesson","story":"Your spam detector passes every test with flying numbers. But Command asks one final, harder question: fair to WHOM? A good AI cadet checks not just if a model is accurate, but whether it is honest, fair, and open about how it was tested.","teach":[{"title":"Bias in Evaluation","body":"Bias happens when a model performs unfairly well or poorly for certain groups because the training or test data wasn't representative of everyone. A model can look accurate overall while quietly failing one group of people.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Transparency","body":"Transparency means being open about how a model was tested, what data was used, and what its real metrics are. People affected by a model's decisions deserve to understand how reliable it actually is, not just be told 'trust the AI'.","visual":"🔎"},{"title":"Accuracy Can Be Misleading","body":"A high accuracy score can hide serious problems, like missing rare but important cases (false negatives). It is an ethical duty to report precision, recall, and F1 honestly, instead of only showing the accuracy number that looks best.","visual":"🎭"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why can a high 'accuracy' score sometimes be misleading on an imbalanced dataset, like rare disease detection?","options":["A model could ignore the rare cases entirely and still score high accuracy by predicting the majority class","Accuracy can only ever be calculated for text data","High accuracy always means a model is completely perfect","Accuracy scores can never be written as a percentage"],"answer":0,"explain":"If positives are rare, a model can score high accuracy while still missing almost every real positive case."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is 'bias' in the context of evaluating AI models?","options":["When a model performs unfairly well or poorly for certain groups because the data wasn't representative","When a model runs slightly slower than expected","When a model uses a large amount of storage space","When training simply takes longer than one day"],"answer":0,"explain":"Bias in evaluation often traces back to data that doesn't fairly represent all groups the model will affect."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does 'transparency' matter when evaluating AI models?","options":["People affected by the model's decisions deserve to understand how it was tested and how reliable it is","It makes the underlying code compile faster","It is a rule that only applies to video game AI","It automatically reduces the size of the dataset"],"answer":0,"explain":"Being transparent about testing methods and results allows people to trust, question, or challenge a model's decisions."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A face-detection model is tested on 200 photos: 180 lighter-skin photos (171 correct) and 20 darker-skin photos (9 correct). What is the model's OVERALL accuracy as a decimal?","answer":0.9,"explain":"Total correct = 171 + 9 = 180. Overall accuracy = 180 / 200 = 0.9."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the scenario above, the model got 171/180 right on lighter-skin photos but only 9/20 right on darker-skin photos, even though overall accuracy looked fine at 0.9. What ethical concern does this reveal?","options":["The model may be biased, performing far worse on one group despite a good overall accuracy score","The model is perfectly fair to every single person","The dataset was simply too large to ever evaluate","The model's processing speed is the only real problem here"],"answer":0,"explain":"A single overall accuracy number can hide a large fairness gap between groups — always check performance across subgroups too."}]},{"id":"em-boss","title":"Boss Battle: Lord Confusio","icon":"👹","kind":"boss","story":"Lord Confusio, Keeper of the Confusion Matrix, blocks your path with a wall of TPs, FPs, TNs, and FNs. Only a cadet who has truly mastered train-test splits, accuracy, error, precision, recall, F1, and the ethics of evaluation can scatter the confusion and pass through.","boss":{"name":"Lord Confusio, Keeper of the Confusion Matrix","taunt":"Four little letters, TP, FP, TN, FN, and already your head is spinning. Let's see if you can out-math me, cadet!"},"teach":[{"title":"Final Review","body":"Remember the chain: split your data (train-test split), measure correctness (accuracy and error), build a confusion matrix (TP, FP, TN, FN), compute sharper metrics (precision, recall, F1), and always check for bias and transparency.","visual":"🧠"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Lord Confusio's matrix: TP=50, FP=10, FN=15, TN=125. What is the TOTAL number of predictions?","answer":200,"explain":"Total = TP + FP + FN + TN = 50 + 10 + 15 + 125 = 200."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the same matrix (TP=50, FP=10, FN=15, TN=125), what is the ACCURACY as a decimal?","answer":0.875,"explain":"Accuracy = (TP+TN)/Total = (50+125)/200 = 175/200 = 0.875."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the same matrix, what is the PRECISION, rounded to 2 decimal places?","answer":0.83,"explain":"Precision = TP/(TP+FP) = 50/(50+10) = 50/60 = 0.8333, which rounds to 0.83."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the same matrix, what is the RECALL, rounded to 2 decimal places?","answer":0.77,"explain":"Recall = TP/(TP+FN) = 50/(50+15) = 50/65 = 0.7692, which rounds to 0.77."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using precision = 5/6 and recall = 10/13 exactly (from the same matrix), what is the F1 score as a decimal?","answer":0.8,"explain":"F1 = 2 x (5/6 x 10/13) / (5/6 + 10/13) = (50/39) / (125/78) = 0.8 exactly."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A dataset of 900 records is split 80/20 (train/test) to fight Lord Confusio fairly. How many records are in the TEST set?","answer":180,"explain":"20% of 900 = 0.2 x 900 = 180 records for the test set."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For a disease-detection model, where missing a sick patient (a false negative) is dangerous, which metric matters MOST?","options":["Recall, because it measures how many actual positive cases were correctly caught","Precision, because it only measures how fast the model runs","The total size of the dataset, regardless of anything else","The color used in the confusion matrix chart"],"answer":0,"explain":"Recall = TP/(TP+FN), so high recall means very few real positive cases (like sick patients) are being missed."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A model scores 95% accuracy on a dataset where 95% of examples are negative, but it has 0% recall on the positive class. What ethical concern does this illustrate?","options":["That relying on accuracy alone can hide bias and poor real-world performance on the class that matters","That the model is completely perfect and totally reliable","That accuracy is mathematically impossible to compute","That the F1 score is not a real or usable metric"],"answer":0,"explain":"A lazy model can reach high accuracy on imbalanced data while being practically useless for detecting the thing that actually matters."}]}]},{"id":"statisticaldata10","index":3,"name":"Statistical Data","subtitle":"Artificial Intelligence — Unit 4","emoji":"🐧","palette":{"from":"#a78bfa","to":"#4c1d95"},"lessons":[{"id":"sd1","title":"The Data Detective","icon":"🔎","kind":"lesson","story":"You wash up on Study Atlas's research island, where a marine biologist's spreadsheets are scattered across the beach. To help her, you'll first need to understand what Data Science actually is — and where it shows up in the real world.","teach":[{"title":"What is Data Science?","body":"Data Science is the field of collecting, cleaning, and analysing data to find patterns and answer questions. It blends statistics, computer science, and knowledge of a specific subject (like biology) to turn raw numbers into useful insight.","visual":"📊"},{"title":"Data Science feeds AI","body":"Before an AI model can predict or classify anything, someone has to gather good data and understand it statistically. Data Science is the groundwork that makes AI models possible — without clean, well-understood data, an AI model has nothing reliable to learn from.","visual":"🧩"},{"title":"Applications all around you","body":"Data Science powers weather forecasts, hospital diagnosis support, cricket team strategy, recommendation feeds on apps, farming yield predictions, and fraud detection in banking. Any field that generates data can use it to find patterns and make better decisions.","visual":"🌍"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which best defines Data Science?","options":["Collecting, cleaning, and analysing data to find patterns and insights","Only writing code for websites","Only drawing charts in a spreadsheet","Repairing computer hardware"],"answer":0,"explain":"Data Science combines statistics, computing, and subject knowledge to turn raw data into insight."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does an AI model need Data Science before it can 'learn'?","options":["Because good, well-understood data is the foundation a model learns patterns from","Because AI models don't actually need any data","Because Data Science replaces the need for AI entirely","Because it makes the model's colours look nicer"],"answer":0,"explain":"AI models learn patterns from data, so the data must first be gathered and understood well."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is a real-world application of Data Science?","options":["Predicting tomorrow's weather from past weather patterns","Sharpening a pencil","Turning on a torch","Boiling water"],"answer":0,"explain":"Weather forecasting analyses huge amounts of past and live data to predict future conditions."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A hospital uses past patient records to help doctors flag high-risk cases early. This is an example of:","options":["Data Science applied to healthcare","A random guess with no data","A purely mechanical task","An application with no connection to statistics"],"answer":0,"explain":"Analysing historical patient data to support decisions is a classic Data Science application."}]},{"id":"sd2","title":"Code, Low-Code, No-Code","icon":"🧱","kind":"lesson","story":"The biologist hands you her laptop: 'I don't know how to program, but I still need to build an AI model for my penguins.' Time to learn how she can do exactly that — without writing a single line of code.","teach":[{"title":"Traditional coding AI","body":"In traditional AI development, a programmer writes code line by line (often in Python) to load data, build a model, and test it. This gives full control and flexibility, but requires strong programming skill.","visual":"⌨️"},{"title":"No-Code AI","body":"No-Code AI lets you build an AI model using visual, drag-and-drop tools — no programming required at all. You connect ready-made blocks (like 'load data' or 'train model') to build a working AI workflow visually.","visual":"🧲"},{"title":"Low-Code AI","body":"Low-Code AI sits in between: most of the work is done visually, but you can add small bits of custom code when you need extra control or a feature the visual blocks don't offer. It's a middle ground between full coding and no-code.","visual":"🪛"},{"title":"Some no-code tools","body":"Popular no-code AI tools include Orange Data Mining (for data analysis and machine learning), Google's Teachable Machine, and various drag-and-drop platforms. These let students, scientists, and businesspeople build AI models without being programmers.","visual":"🛠️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is No-Code AI?","options":["Building AI models using visual, drag-and-drop tools with no programming needed","Writing every line of an AI model's code by hand","AI that has no data at all","A type of computer virus"],"answer":0,"explain":"No-Code AI tools use visual blocks and connections instead of written code."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How does Low-Code AI differ from No-Code AI?","options":["Low-Code mostly uses visual tools but allows some custom code for extra control","Low-Code never uses visual tools at all","Low-Code and No-Code are exactly the same thing","Low-Code requires more coding than traditional programming"],"answer":0,"explain":"Low-Code is a middle ground: mostly visual, with room to add small pieces of code when needed."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the main advantage of No-Code AI tools for someone like a biologist with no programming background?","options":["They can still build and use AI models without learning to code","They must first become expert programmers","They can only look at data, never build models","They need a separate coder to click every button"],"answer":0,"explain":"No-Code tools open up AI model-building to people without programming skills."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is an example of a No-Code AI tool?","options":["Orange Data Mining","A plain text editor with no AI features","A calculator app","A basic word processor"],"answer":0,"explain":"Orange Data Mining is a well-known visual, no-code tool for data analysis and machine learning."}]},{"id":"sd3","title":"Meet the Palmer Penguins","icon":"🐧","kind":"lesson","story":"The biologist's real project appears: a table of measurements from three penguin species on nearby islands. Her question — 'Can an AI tell the species apart just from body measurements?' — is about to become your case study.","teach":[{"title":"The Palmer Penguins dataset","body":"The Palmer Penguins dataset records real measurements of three species — Adelie, Chinstrap, and Gentoo — including bill length, bill depth, flipper length, body mass, island, and sex. It's a popular beginner dataset for practising data science and AI.","visual":"📋"},{"title":"The goal: predict the species","body":"The case study's AI task is classification: given a penguin's measurements, predict which of the three species it belongs to. This is the kind of problem no-code tools like Orange are great for exploring.","visual":"🎯"},{"title":"Why explore data before modeling?","body":"Before training any model, data scientists first study the data statistically — checking for missing values, unusual outliers, and how features differ between species. Skipping this step often leads to a poor or misleading model.","visual":"🧭"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the Palmer Penguins dataset, what is the AI task the case study focuses on?","options":["Classifying a penguin's species from its body measurements","Predicting tomorrow's stock prices","Translating penguin sounds into English","Counting the number of islands in the world"],"answer":0,"explain":"The case study trains a model to classify penguins into species (Adelie, Chinstrap, Gentoo) using their measurements."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is NOT one of the three penguin species in the Palmer Penguins dataset?","options":["Emperor","Adelie","Chinstrap","Gentoo"],"answer":0,"explain":"The dataset covers Adelie, Chinstrap, and Gentoo penguins — Emperor penguins are not part of it."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why should you explore and understand data statistically before building an AI model with it?","options":["To catch missing values, outliers, and patterns that affect how well the model will work","Because it makes the file size smaller","Because models refuse to run on unexplored data","It isn't actually necessary — you can skip straight to modeling"],"answer":0,"explain":"Exploring data first helps you spot problems and understand feature patterns before trusting a model built on it."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is a feature (measurement) recorded for each penguin in the dataset?","options":["Flipper length","Favourite colour","Number of siblings","Top swimming speed"],"answer":0,"explain":"The dataset records bill length, bill depth, flipper length, body mass, island, and sex for each penguin."}]},{"id":"sd4","title":"Numbers That Tell a Story","icon":"➗","kind":"lesson","story":"The biologist points to a column of flipper-length measurements: 'Before you show me a fancy model, tell me — what's typical for these penguins?' Statistics is how you answer that.","teach":[{"title":"Mean (average)","body":"The mean is the sum of all values divided by how many values there are. It gives a quick sense of the 'typical' value in a dataset, which data scientists check before modeling.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"Median (the middle value)","body":"The median is the middle value when data is sorted from smallest to largest. It's useful because, unlike the mean, it isn't thrown off much by one unusually large or small value.","visual":"🎯"},{"title":"Mode (the most common value)","body":"The mode is the value that appears most often in a dataset. It's especially useful for categorical data, like which island most penguins were found on.","visual":"🔁"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"5 Adelie penguins have flipper lengths (mm): 181, 186, 190, 193, 195. What is the mean flipper length?","answer":189,"explain":"(181+186+190+193+195) = 945, and 945 / 5 = 189 mm."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the same 5 flipper lengths (181, 186, 190, 193, 195 mm), what is the median?","answer":190,"explain":"Sorted, the values are 181, 186, 190, 193, 195 — the middle (3rd) value is 190."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A set of 5 penguin bill lengths (mm) is: 39, 40, 40, 42, 44. What is the mode?","options":["40","39","42","44"],"answer":0,"explain":"40 appears twice, more than any other value, so it is the mode."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why might a data scientist prefer the median over the mean for a dataset with one extreme outlier?","options":["The median isn't pulled far off by a single unusually high or low value","The median always equals the mode","The mean can never be calculated with outliers present","The median ignores all the data except the first value"],"answer":0,"explain":"Because the median only depends on the middle position, one extreme value barely affects it."}]},{"id":"sd5","title":"Spread and Outliers","icon":"📏","kind":"lesson","story":"One penguin's flipper measurement looks way too large — a typo, or a genuinely huge bird? You'll need to understand spread and outliers to decide.","teach":[{"title":"Range: how spread out is the data?","body":"The range is the difference between the highest and lowest value in a dataset (Range = Max − Min). A small range means values are clustered close together; a large range means they're spread far apart.","visual":"↔️"},{"title":"Minimum and Maximum","body":"The minimum and maximum are simply the smallest and largest values in the dataset. Data scientists check these first, because an impossible min or max (like a negative flipper length) usually signals a data-entry error.","visual":"🔺"},{"title":"Outliers","body":"An outlier is a value that is unusually far from the rest of the data. Outliers can be genuine (a very large Gentoo penguin) or mistakes (a typo), so data scientists investigate them before deciding whether to keep or remove them.","visual":"🚩"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the flipper lengths (mm) 181, 186, 190, 193, 195, what is the range?","answer":14,"explain":"Range = Max − Min = 195 − 181 = 14 mm."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A dataset of Gentoo penguin flipper lengths mostly ranges from 210-231 mm, but one entry reads 21 mm. What should a data scientist do?","options":["Flag it as a likely outlier or data-entry error and investigate it","Immediately trust it as the new typical value","Delete the entire dataset","Ignore it completely and never check any other values"],"answer":0,"explain":"A value far outside the normal range should be checked — it's likely a mistake (like a missing digit) rather than a real measurement."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does a small range in a dataset tell you?","options":["The values are clustered close together","Every value is identical to the mode","The dataset has no minimum value","The mean cannot be calculated"],"answer":0,"explain":"A small range means the difference between the highest and lowest value is small, so the data points sit close together."}]},{"id":"sd6","title":"Inside Orange Data Mining","icon":"🍊","kind":"lesson","story":"The biologist opens a bright orange application on her laptop. 'This is how I explore my penguins without coding,' she says, dragging colourful boxes onto a blank canvas.","teach":[{"title":"What is Orange Data Mining?","body":"Orange is a free, open-source no-code tool for data analysis and machine learning. Instead of writing code, you build a workflow visually by dragging 'widgets' onto a canvas and connecting them with wires.","visual":"🍊"},{"title":"Widgets and workflows","body":"Each widget performs one job — for example, a File widget loads data, a Data Table widget displays it, and a Scatter Plot widget visualises it. Connecting widgets in sequence builds a complete data-analysis workflow.","visual":"🔗"},{"title":"Data exploration in Orange","body":"Before modeling, Orange offers widgets like Data Table, Distributions, and Scatter Plot to explore the Palmer Penguins data — for instance, seeing whether flipper length clearly separates the three species visually.","visual":"🔬"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is Orange Data Mining?","options":["A free, open-source no-code tool for data analysis and machine learning","A programming language like Python","A type of penguin species","A spreadsheet formula"],"answer":0,"explain":"Orange is a visual, no-code tool used to explore data and build machine learning workflows."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How do you build a workflow in Orange?","options":["By dragging widgets onto a canvas and connecting them with wires","By typing Python scripts into a terminal","By writing HTML pages","By recording a video"],"answer":0,"explain":"Orange uses visual programming: widgets are dragged onto a canvas and linked together to form a workflow."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which Orange widget would you use to see the actual rows and columns of the Palmer Penguins data?","options":["Data Table","Neural Network","Confusion Matrix","Test and Score"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Data Table widget displays the raw dataset as rows and columns, useful for initial exploration."}]},{"id":"sd7","title":"From Explore to Evaluate","icon":"🧪","kind":"lesson","story":"Data explored, patterns spotted — now it's time to let Orange build an actual model that guesses a penguin's species, and check whether it can be trusted.","teach":[{"title":"The AI project cycle, no-code style","body":"The AI project cycle — data exploration, modeling, and evaluation — applies just as much inside a no-code tool as it does in written code. In Orange, each stage is simply handled by connecting the right widgets instead of writing functions.","visual":"🔄"},{"title":"Modeling stage","body":"In the modeling stage, you connect the explored penguin data to a learner widget (such as Tree or kNN) that studies the measurements and species labels to learn a pattern for telling the species apart.","visual":"🌳"},{"title":"Evaluation stage","body":"In the evaluation stage, a widget like Test and Score checks how accurately the trained model predicts species it hasn't seen before, often showing a Confusion Matrix comparing predicted vs actual species.","visual":"✅"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the no-code AI project cycle applied to Orange, which stage comes first?","options":["Data exploration","Evaluation","Modeling","Deployment to a phone app"],"answer":0,"explain":"You explore and understand the data first, before training or evaluating a model on it."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In Orange's modeling stage for the penguin case study, what does a learner widget (like Tree or kNN) do?","options":["Studies the measurements and species labels to learn a pattern that tells species apart","Deletes the dataset","Only displays a bar chart, nothing else","Converts the data into a spreadsheet file"],"answer":0,"explain":"A learner widget is where the actual machine learning happens — it learns patterns from the training data."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does the evaluation stage (e.g. the Test and Score widget) tell you about a trained model?","options":["How accurately it predicts on data it hasn't seen before","How many widgets are on the canvas","The exact colour of each penguin","How to delete the model"],"answer":0,"explain":"Evaluation measures the model's performance, usually by testing it on data separate from what it trained on."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which no-code widget output would help you see how many Gentoo penguins were mistakenly predicted as Chinstrap?","options":["Confusion Matrix","File widget","Data Table","Scatter Plot alone"],"answer":0,"explain":"A Confusion Matrix shows predicted vs actual classes, revealing exactly which species get mixed up."}]},{"id":"sd-boss","title":"The Statistics Sentinel","icon":"🛡️","kind":"boss","story":"At the edge of the research station stands the Statistics Sentinel, guardian of the penguin archive. It will only let you pass if you can prove you understand Data Science, no-code AI, statistics, and the Orange workflow — all at once.","boss":{"name":"The Statistics Sentinel","taunt":"Numbers don't lie, but data detectives who skip their stats sure do stumble!"},"teach":[{"title":"Everything you've learned","body":"Data Science turns raw data into insight, no-code tools like Orange let you build AI models visually, statistics like mean/median/mode/range help you understand data before modeling, and the AI project cycle (explore, model, evaluate) applies to no-code workflows too.","visual":"🐧"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is Data Science, in one line?","options":["Collecting, cleaning, and analysing data to find patterns and insights","Only writing website code","A brand of laptop","A synonym for hardware repair"],"answer":0,"explain":"Data Science combines statistics, computing, and domain knowledge to extract insight from data."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What best describes No-Code AI?","options":["Building AI models with visual, drag-and-drop tools and no programming","Writing every line of the model's code by hand","A tool that only works with penguins","AI that requires no data at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"No-Code AI tools remove the need to write code by using visual blocks and connections."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How is Low-Code AI different from No-Code AI?","options":["Low-Code allows some custom code alongside mostly visual tools","Low-Code never uses any visual tools","Low-Code and No-Code mean exactly the same thing","Low-Code requires more code than traditional programming"],"answer":0,"explain":"Low-Code sits between full coding and no-code: mostly visual, with room for small custom code additions."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"5 penguin flipper lengths (mm) are: 181, 186, 190, 193, 195. What is the mean?","answer":189,"explain":"945 total divided by 5 values = 189 mm."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the same 5 flipper lengths (181, 186, 190, 193, 195 mm), what is the range?","answer":14,"explain":"Range = Max − Min = 195 − 181 = 14 mm."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In Orange Data Mining, how do you build an analysis workflow?","options":["By dragging widgets onto a canvas and connecting them with wires","By typing Python scripts","By emailing the biologist","By printing the dataset on paper"],"answer":0,"explain":"Orange uses visual programming with widgets connected together, not written code."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which stage of the AI project cycle happens right before evaluation?","options":["Modeling","Deployment","Data exploration","Nothing, evaluation is always first"],"answer":0,"explain":"The cycle flows: data exploration, then modeling (training), then evaluation (testing the trained model)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does a Confusion Matrix in the evaluation stage help you see?","options":["How predicted species compare to actual species, including mistakes","The exact weight of each individual penguin","The island each penguin lives on","How many widgets exist in Orange"],"answer":0,"explain":"A Confusion Matrix lays out predicted vs actual classes so you can see exactly where the model gets confused."}]}]},{"id":"computervision10","index":4,"name":"Computer Vision","subtitle":"Artificial Intelligence — Unit 5","emoji":"👁️","palette":{"from":"#22d3ee","to":"#164e63"},"lessons":[{"id":"cv1","title":"The Awakening Eye","icon":"👁️","kind":"lesson","story":"You step through a shimmering portal into the Vision Vault, where ancient sensor-golems have gone blind. The Vault Keeper begs you to restore their sight by learning how machines truly 'see' the world.","teach":[{"title":"What is Computer Vision?","body":"Computer Vision (CV) is a field of Artificial Intelligence that trains computers to interpret and understand visual information -- images and videos -- almost the way humans do with their eyes and brain.","visual":"🧠"},{"title":"Real-World Applications","body":"CV powers face unlock on phones, self-driving cars that spot pedestrians, doctors scanning X-rays for disease, factories checking products for defects, and farmers monitoring crop health from drone photos.","visual":"🚗"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does Computer Vision enable a computer to do?","options":["Interpret and understand images and videos","Only store photos without understanding them","Print documents faster","Translate spoken languages"],"answer":0,"explain":"Computer Vision is the AI field focused on making computers interpret and understand visual data."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is a real-world application of Computer Vision?","options":["Unlocking a phone by recognizing your face","Adding two numbers together","Sending a text message","Charging a battery"],"answer":0,"explain":"Face unlock uses computer vision to recognize a person's face from a camera image."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Computer Vision is a field of which broader area of technology?","options":["Artificial Intelligence","Accounting","Civil Engineering","Music Production"],"answer":0,"explain":"Computer Vision is one of the major fields within Artificial Intelligence."}]},{"id":"cv2","title":"Three Trials of Sight","icon":"🎯","kind":"lesson","story":"The Vault Keeper hands you three enchanted lenses. Each reveals a different power: naming a whole picture, pointing out objects within it, or tracing their exact outlines. Master all three to unlock the next chamber.","teach":[{"title":"Image Classification","body":"Classification looks at an entire image and assigns it ONE label -- like deciding a photo shows 'a dog' or 'a cat'. It answers the question: what is this image, overall?","visual":"🏷️"},{"title":"Object Detection","body":"Detection goes further: it finds WHERE objects are and draws a bounding box around each one, labelling every object it finds -- even if there are several different objects in one picture.","visual":"📦"},{"title":"Image Segmentation","body":"Segmentation is the most detailed task -- it labels every single pixel, tracing the exact outline/shape of each object instead of just a rough box. It's used in things like self-driving cars mapping road edges precisely.","visual":"✂️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which CV task assigns a single label to an entire image?","options":["Image Classification","Object Detection","Image Segmentation","Image Compression"],"answer":0,"explain":"Classification gives one overall label to the whole image."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which CV task draws a bounding box around each object AND labels it?","options":["Object Detection","Image Classification","Pixel Resolution","Grayscale Conversion"],"answer":0,"explain":"Object detection locates objects with bounding boxes and labels each one."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which CV task labels every individual pixel to trace an object's exact outline?","options":["Image Segmentation","Image Classification","Object Detection","Pixel Sampling"],"answer":0,"explain":"Segmentation classifies every pixel, giving a precise outline of each object."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A photo has both a cat AND a dog. A system draws a box around each and labels them 'cat' and 'dog'. Which task is this?","options":["Object Detection","Image Classification","Segmentation only","None of these"],"answer":0,"explain":"Drawing labeled bounding boxes around multiple objects is object detection."}]},{"id":"cv3","title":"The Pixel Grid","icon":"🔲","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in the Vault, you find a giant mosaic wall made of tiny glowing tiles. The Keeper explains: every digital image is secretly built from thousands of these tiny tiles -- and you must learn to count and read them.","teach":[{"title":"What is a Pixel?","body":"A pixel (short for 'picture element') is the smallest single point/unit of a digital image -- like one tiny colored square in a mosaic. Millions of pixels together form a full picture.","visual":"🟦"},{"title":"Resolution","body":"Resolution tells you how many pixels an image contains, usually written as width x height (e.g., 1920 x 1080). Higher resolution means more pixels, so the image can show finer detail.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Pixel Value","body":"Each pixel stores a number called its pixel value, representing brightness or intensity. In a standard 8-bit image, this value ranges from 0 (darkest/black) to 255 (brightest/white).","visual":"🔢"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is a pixel?","options":["The smallest single point/unit that makes up a digital image","A type of computer virus","A unit of sound volume","A brand of camera"],"answer":0,"explain":"A pixel (picture element) is the smallest building block of a digital image."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An image has a resolution of 10 pixels wide by 8 pixels tall. How many total pixels does the image contain?","answer":80,"explain":"10 x 8 = 80 pixels total."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In a standard 8-bit grayscale image, what is the maximum possible pixel value?","answer":255,"explain":"8-bit pixel values range from 0 to 255, so 255 is the maximum (brightest)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does 'resolution' describe about an image?","options":["The total number of pixels it contains (width x height)","The price of the camera used","The file format, like .jpg or .png","The speed at which the image loads"],"answer":0,"explain":"Resolution is the count of pixels in an image, given as width x height."}]},{"id":"cv4","title":"Colors of the Vault","icon":"🌈","kind":"lesson","story":"A wall of shifting light blocks your path -- some torches burn in ghostly shades of gray, others blaze with full color. To pass, you must understand how machines store both kinds of images as numbers.","teach":[{"title":"Grayscale Images","body":"A grayscale image has just ONE value per pixel (no separate color channels), ranging from 0 (black) to 255 (white), with everything in between being a shade of gray.","visual":"⚫"},{"title":"RGB Images","body":"A color RGB image stores THREE values per pixel -- Red, Green, and Blue -- each from 0 to 255. Mixing different amounts of these three channels of light can create millions of different colors.","visual":"🔴"},{"title":"Mixing RGB Values","body":"Combining Red=255, Green=0, Blue=0 gives pure red. Red=255, Green=255, Blue=255 gives white, and Red=0, Green=0, Blue=0 gives black -- because all channels have zero light.","visual":"🎨"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How many values does a grayscale image store per pixel?","options":["One (0 to 255)","Three (Red, Green, Blue)","Four channels","Zero -- grayscale has no values"],"answer":0,"explain":"Grayscale images store a single brightness value per pixel, from 0 to 255."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How many channels/values does an RGB image store per pixel?","options":["Three -- Red, Green, and Blue","One value only","Ten values","Two values"],"answer":0,"explain":"RGB images store three values per pixel, one each for Red, Green, and Blue."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What color results from RGB values Red=255, Green=255, Blue=0?","options":["Yellow","Black","Pure Blue","White"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mixing full Red and Green light (with no Blue) produces yellow."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An RGB image is 20 pixels wide and 10 pixels tall. How many individual channel values (R, G, and B combined) are stored in total?","answer":600,"explain":"20 x 10 = 200 pixels; each pixel stores 3 values (R,G,B), so 200 x 3 = 600 values."}]},{"id":"cv5","title":"Tools Without Code","icon":"🧰","kind":"lesson","story":"The Vault's workshop is filled with strange looking machines that build AI models without a single line of code. An apprentice offers to show you three of the most powerful: Lobe, Teachable Machine, and Orange Data Mining.","teach":[{"title":"Lobe","body":"Lobe is a free, no-code tool that lets you train your own image-classification AI model just by dragging in labeled photos -- no programming needed -- and then export the trained model to use elsewhere.","visual":"🖼️"},{"title":"Teachable Machine","body":"Google's Teachable Machine is a free web-based tool where you can train a model using your webcam, images, or sounds, simply by showing it examples of each category -- great for quick projects like a 'Smart Sorter'.","visual":"📷"},{"title":"Orange Data Mining","body":"Orange is a visual, drag-and-drop data mining and machine learning tool where you connect 'widgets' into a workflow instead of writing code -- useful for building real classification projects, such as detecting coral bleaching from images.","visual":"🧩"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the main idea behind 'no-code' AI tools like Lobe and Teachable Machine?","options":["They let you build AI models without writing programming code","They only work without any computer","They automatically write code for you to copy","They require advanced coding certifications"],"answer":0,"explain":"No-code tools let you build and train AI models through drag-and-drop interfaces instead of code."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which no-code tool lets you quickly train a model using your webcam, images, or sounds?","options":["Teachable Machine","Orange Data Mining","Lobe","None of these"],"answer":0,"explain":"Teachable Machine is Google's browser-based tool for training models with webcam, image, or sound examples."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which tool uses a visual, drag-and-drop 'widget' workflow for data mining and machine learning projects?","options":["Orange Data Mining","Teachable Machine","Lobe","Piskel"],"answer":0,"explain":"Orange Data Mining connects widgets in a visual workflow to build ML projects, like the coral bleaching classifier."}]},{"id":"cv6","title":"The Kernel's Slide","icon":"🧮","kind":"lesson","story":"A guardian spirit made of shifting numbers blocks the corridor. It only lets travelers pass who can slide a small magic square -- called a kernel -- across a grid of pixels and correctly compute what appears.","teach":[{"title":"What is Convolution?","body":"Convolution is a mathematical operation used to extract features from an image. A small matrix called a kernel (or filter) slides across the image; at each position, it multiplies its values with the pixel values underneath and adds up the results to get one output number.","visual":"🔍"},{"title":"Why Use Convolution?","body":"Different kernels can highlight different features -- some detect edges, some blur the image, some sharpen it. This makes convolution a key feature-extraction technique in computer vision.","visual":"✨"},{"title":"A Simple Example","body":"Take pixel patch [[1,2],[3,4]] and kernel [[0,1],[1,0]]. Multiply matching positions and sum: (1x0)+(2x1)+(3x1)+(4x0) = 0+2+3+0 = 5. That single number becomes one value in the new 'feature map'.","visual":"➕"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does a convolution operation do to extract features from an image?","options":["Slides a small kernel over the image, multiplying and summing overlapping values","Deletes random pixels from the image","Sorts pixels from brightest to darkest","Converts the image into text"],"answer":0,"explain":"Convolution slides a kernel across the image, multiplying and summing overlapping values to build a feature map."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using pixel patch [[1,2],[3,4]] and kernel [[0,1],[1,0]], compute the convolution result: multiply matching positions and add them up.","answer":5,"explain":"(1x0)+(2x1)+(3x1)+(4x0) = 0+2+3+0 = 5."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 3x3 patch has all pixel values equal to 20 (a flat, uniform area). An edge-detection kernel has a center value of 8 and all eight surrounding values of -1. What is the convolution result (sum of elementwise products)?","answer":0,"explain":"Center: 20x8=160. Eight surrounding cells: 20x(-1) each = -20, times 8 = -160. Total: 160 + (-160) = 0 -- flat areas give a near-zero response with edge kernels, which is how convolution highlights edges instead of flat regions."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In computer vision, the small matrix that slides across an image during convolution is called a:","options":["Kernel (or filter)","Pixel","Resolution","Label"],"answer":0,"explain":"This small sliding matrix is called a kernel, also known as a filter."}]},{"id":"cv7","title":"The Learning Lenses","icon":"🕸️","kind":"lesson","story":"Beyond the guardian, you find an enormous crystalline network of lenses stacked on top of each other -- a Convolutional Neural Network. Each layer of lenses sees something a little more complex than the layer before it.","teach":[{"title":"What is a CNN?","body":"A Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) is a deep learning architecture specially designed for image data. Instead of using hand-picked kernels, a CNN automatically LEARNS the best kernels during training.","visual":"🕸️"},{"title":"Convolutional Layers","body":"A convolutional layer applies many learned kernels across the input image, producing multiple 'feature maps' -- each one highlighting a different pattern, like edges, textures, or corners.","visual":"🗂️"},{"title":"Deeper Layers, Bigger Ideas","body":"Early convolutional layers detect simple features like edges. As you go deeper into the network, layers combine these into more complex features -- from edges to shapes to entire objects like eyes or wheels.","visual":"🏗️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is a CNN (Convolutional Neural Network) specially designed to process?","options":["Image data","Only text data","Only audio files","Spreadsheet numbers"],"answer":0,"explain":"CNNs are a deep learning architecture built specifically for processing image data."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a CNN, where do the kernels (filters) used in convolutional layers come from?","options":["They are automatically learned by the network during training","They must always be typed in manually by a programmer","They are randomly deleted after each use","They come from a printed textbook"],"answer":0,"explain":"CNNs learn the best kernel values automatically from training data."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"As a CNN's convolutional layers go deeper, what generally happens to the features they detect?","options":["They become more complex, combining simple features into shapes and objects","They become simpler and disappear entirely","They turn into plain text","They stop processing the image"],"answer":0,"explain":"Deeper layers combine simple features like edges into more complex shapes and objects."}]},{"id":"cv8","title":"The Pooling Chamber","icon":"🏊","kind":"lesson","story":"One last chamber remains: a shrinking room that compresses everything to its essence without losing what matters. This is the Pooling Chamber, where a CNN learns to focus only on the strongest signals.","teach":[{"title":"Pooling Layers","body":"A pooling layer shrinks (downsamples) each feature map, keeping the most important information while reducing the amount of data the network must process. This makes CNNs faster and less likely to overfit.","visual":"📉"},{"title":"Max Pooling","body":"The most common type, max pooling, looks at a small region (e.g., 2x2 pixels) and keeps only the largest value from that region, discarding the rest.","visual":"🔝"},{"title":"Why CNNs Excel at Recognizing Images","body":"CNNs share the same learned kernel across the whole image (fewer parameters to learn), preserve the spatial relationships between nearby pixels, and stay accurate even when an object shifts slightly -- making them extremely effective at recognizing images.","visual":"🏆"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does a pooling layer do in a CNN?","options":["Shrinks feature maps while keeping the most important information","Adds more pixels to the image","Deletes the entire image","Converts the image to grayscale"],"answer":0,"explain":"Pooling downsamples feature maps, keeping key information and reducing data size."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using 2x2 max pooling on the region [[3,7],[2,9]], what single value is kept?","answer":9,"explain":"Max pooling keeps only the largest value in the region: max(3,7,2,9) = 9."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why are CNNs especially good at recognizing images, compared to a plain fully-connected network?","options":["They share learned kernels across the image and preserve spatial relationships between pixels","They ignore all pixel values completely","They only work on black-and-white images","They require no training data at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"Weight sharing via kernels and preserving spatial structure make CNNs efficient and accurate on images."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is a typical basic order of layers in a simple CNN?","options":["Convolution -> Pooling -> Fully Connected -> Output","Output -> Input -> Pooling","Pooling -> Output -> Convolution","Fully Connected -> Input -> Kernel"],"answer":0,"explain":"A simple CNN typically extracts features with convolution, downsamples with pooling, then classifies with fully connected layers to produce an output."}]},{"id":"cv-boss","title":"Guardian of the Vision Vault","icon":"👁️‍🗨️","kind":"boss","story":"The Vault Keeper's final guardian rises -- a colossal being built entirely of pixels, kernels, and glowing feature maps. Prove you have truly mastered Computer Vision, or be reset back to a blur of static.","boss":{"name":"The Pixel Sentinel","taunt":"You think you can SEE through my defenses? Every pixel of me disagrees!"},"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does Computer Vision allow a computer to do?","options":["Interpret and understand images and videos","Only save files to disk","Play music files","Send emails automatically"],"answer":0,"explain":"Computer Vision is the AI field that lets computers interpret and understand visual data."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which CV task labels an entire image with a single tag?","options":["Image Classification","Object Detection","Image Segmentation","Pooling"],"answer":0,"explain":"Classification assigns one overall label to the whole image."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An image has a resolution of 12 pixels wide by 5 pixels tall. How many total pixels does it contain?","answer":60,"explain":"12 x 5 = 60."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How many values (channels) does one pixel need in an RGB image?","options":["Three (Red, Green, Blue)","One","Five","Zero"],"answer":0,"explain":"RGB images store three channel values per pixel: Red, Green, and Blue."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the maximum pixel value in an 8-bit grayscale image?","answer":255,"explain":"8-bit values range from 0 to 255."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using pixel patch [[2,1],[0,3]] and kernel [[1,0],[0,1]], what is the convolution result (multiply matching positions, then sum)?","answer":5,"explain":"(2x1)+(1x0)+(0x0)+(3x1) = 2+0+0+3 = 5."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a CNN, what does a pooling layer (like max pooling) do?","options":["Shrinks feature maps by keeping only the most important values","Adds new color channels to the image","Deletes the neural network","Converts pixels into audio"],"answer":0,"explain":"Pooling downsamples feature maps, keeping the strongest/most important values."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why are CNNs particularly effective at recognizing images?","options":["They share learned kernels and preserve spatial relationships between pixels","They avoid using any pixel data","They only work on single-pixel images","They require the image to be converted to text first"],"answer":0,"explain":"Weight-sharing kernels and preserved spatial structure make CNNs efficient and accurate at recognizing images."}]}]},{"id":"nlp10","index":5,"name":"Natural Language Processing","subtitle":"Artificial Intelligence — Unit 6","emoji":"💬","palette":{"from":"#fb923c","to":"#7c2d12"},"lessons":[{"id":"nl1","title":"Why Machines Struggle With Our Words","icon":"🧩","kind":"lesson","story":"You've just switched on a brand-new translator bot for the class trip abroad. It hears 'That test was sick!' and confidently translates it as 'That exam made me ill.' Time to teach it what human language really looks like.","teach":[{"title":"Language Is Full of Ambiguity","body":"Many words have more than one meaning, and machines can't always tell which one you mean. 'Bank' could mean a riverbank or a place that keeps your money — only the surrounding context tells you which.","visual":"🏦"},{"title":"Meaning Depends on Context and Tone","body":"The same sentence can mean different things depending on who says it, when, and how. Sarcasm like 'Oh great, another Monday' means the opposite of what the words literally say, and humans catch that instantly while machines often don't.","visual":"🎭"},{"title":"Grammar Has Countless Exceptions","body":"Natural languages are full of irregular rules, exceptions and multiple correct ways to say the same thing, unlike the strict, fixed rules of a programming language. This makes it hard to write simple rules a computer can always follow.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Vocabulary Keeps Growing and Changing","body":"New words, slang and short forms appear every day — 'selfie', 'lol', 'ghosting' — and the same idea can be expressed with many different synonyms. A machine must constantly keep up with this ever-changing vocabulary.","visual":"🆕"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is the word 'bank' tricky for an NLP program?","options":["It can mean different things, like a riverbank or a money bank","It is too short for computers to read","It only exists in the English language","Computers cannot store the letter 'b'"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is lexical ambiguity — one word, more than one possible meaning, decided only by context."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A friend types 'Wow, I LOVE waiting in traffic' after being stuck for an hour. What makes this hard for a machine to interpret correctly?","options":["It is sarcasm — the real meaning is the opposite of the literal words","The sentence has no verb","The words are misspelled","It is written in a foreign language"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sarcasm relies on tone and context, which are easy for humans to sense but hard for machines to detect from text alone."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these best explains why natural languages are harder for computers than programming languages?","options":["Natural languages have many exceptions, multiple correct forms, and depend on context","Natural languages use only numbers","Programming languages have infinite vocabulary","Natural languages never change over time"],"answer":0,"explain":"Programming languages follow strict, fixed rules; natural languages are flexible, ambiguous and constantly evolving."}]},{"id":"nl2","title":"What Exactly Is NLP?","icon":"🤖","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in the Study Atlas server room, a dusty terminal blinks awake. 'SYSTEM ONLINE. TEACH ME TO UNDERSTAND YOU,' it types. Time to explain what NLP actually does.","teach":[{"title":"Defining NLP","body":"Natural Language Processing (NLP) is a branch of Artificial Intelligence that gives computers the ability to read, understand and work with human languages such as English or Hindi — the way we naturally speak and write, not special computer code.","visual":"💻"},{"title":"A Meeting of Two Worlds","body":"NLP brings together computer science and linguistics, the study of language. Linguists describe how human language works, and computer scientists turn that knowledge into algorithms a machine can run.","visual":"🔗"},{"title":"Understanding and Generating","body":"NLP has two big jobs: Natural Language Understanding (NLU), which is making sense of what a human said or wrote, and Natural Language Generation (NLG), which is producing natural-sounding human language as a reply or summary.","visual":"🔄"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is Natural Language Processing (NLP)?","options":["A branch of AI that helps computers understand and work with human languages","A programming language used only by robots","A tool that only translates numbers into words","A type of computer hardware"],"answer":0,"explain":"NLP is the field of AI focused on human language — reading, understanding and generating it."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"NLP is built by combining ideas from which two fields?","options":["Linguistics and computer science","Chemistry and geography","Music and history","Physics and art"],"answer":0,"explain":"Linguistics explains how language works; computer science turns that into working algorithms."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"When a voice assistant figures out what you asked, that is an example of which NLP task?","options":["Natural Language Understanding (NLU)","Natural Language Generation (NLG)","Text encryption","Image recognition"],"answer":0,"explain":"NLU is about making sense of the human input; NLG would be used when the assistant replies back in words."}]},{"id":"nl3","title":"NLP All Around Us","icon":"🌍","kind":"lesson","story":"Your phone transcribes your voice note into text, suggests captions for your video, and translates a friend's message from French — all before breakfast. Let's map out where NLP is quietly working.","teach":[{"title":"Voice Assistants & Auto Captions","body":"Voice assistants like Siri, Alexa and Google Assistant use NLP to understand spoken commands and reply in natural language. The same speech-to-text skills automatically generate captions and subtitles for videos and live calls.","visual":"🎤"},{"title":"Translation & Sentiment Analysis","body":"Language translation tools like Google Translate use NLP to convert text from one language to another. Sentiment analysis uses NLP to detect whether a piece of text — like a product review — expresses a positive, negative or neutral opinion.","visual":"🌐"},{"title":"Text Classification & Keyword Extraction","body":"Text classification sorts text into categories automatically, such as marking an email as spam or not-spam. Keyword extraction picks out the most important words or phrases from a large piece of text, helping summarise what it's about.","visual":"🏷"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"An email app automatically moves a message to the Spam folder. Which NLP application is this?","options":["Text classification","Keyword extraction","Language translation","Auto captioning"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sorting text into predefined categories, like spam vs. not-spam, is text classification."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A company scans thousands of product reviews to see if customers feel mostly positive or negative about a new phone. Which NLP application are they using?","options":["Sentiment analysis","Speech recognition only","Keyword extraction only","Handwriting recognition"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sentiment analysis detects the emotion or opinion — positive, negative or neutral — expressed in text."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is NOT a typical real-life application of NLP mentioned for everyday use?","options":["Predicting stock market crashes from weather data","Voice assistants understanding spoken commands","Auto-generated video captions","Translating text between languages"],"answer":0,"explain":"Voice assistants, captions and translation are classic NLP applications; predicting stock crashes from weather isn't an NLP task at all."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Pulling out the 3-4 most important words from a long news article to quickly show what it's about is called…","options":["Keyword extraction","Sentence segmentation","Stemming","Tokenization"],"answer":0,"explain":"Keyword extraction identifies the most significant words or phrases in a text."}]},{"id":"nl4","title":"Stage 1 & 2: Breaking Down a Sentence","icon":"🔤","kind":"lesson","story":"The language decoder in the Atlas lab lights up as you feed it a sentence: 'The cat sat on the mat.' Before it can understand anything, it must first break the sentence apart, word by word, rule by rule.","teach":[{"title":"Lexical Analysis: Meet the Words","body":"The first stage of NLP is lexical analysis. The input text is broken into individual words or tokens, and each word's basic meaning is identified, often by looking it up like a dictionary entry — without yet worrying about the whole sentence.","visual":"🔤"},{"title":"Syntax Analysis: Checking the Grammar","body":"The second stage is syntax, or syntactic, analysis. It checks whether the words are arranged in a grammatically correct order and studies how the words relate to each other in the sentence — for example, which word is the subject and which is the verb.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Grammatically Right, But Not Yet Understood","body":"After syntax analysis, a computer knows a sentence is grammatically well formed, but it still doesn't know if the sentence actually makes sense — that job belongs to the next stages.","visual":"✅"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which NLP stage is responsible for breaking a sentence into individual words and looking up their basic meaning?","options":["Lexical analysis","Syntax analysis","Semantic analysis","Logical analysis"],"answer":0,"explain":"Lexical analysis works at the level of individual words, like scanning a dictionary."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which NLP stage checks whether a sentence follows correct grammatical structure, like subject-verb order?","options":["Syntax analysis","Lexical analysis","Keyword extraction","Text classification"],"answer":0,"explain":"Syntax analysis studies grammar and how words are arranged and related in a sentence."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The sentence 'Cat the mat on sat' would most likely fail which stage of NLP?","options":["Syntax analysis, because the word order is grammatically incorrect","Lexical analysis, because the words don't exist","Sentiment analysis, because it is too emotional","Keyword extraction, because it has no keywords"],"answer":0,"explain":"The words themselves are valid, but their order breaks grammatical rules — a syntax problem."}]},{"id":"nl5","title":"Stage 3 & 4: Finding the Real Meaning","icon":"🧠","kind":"lesson","story":"The decoder now shows a grammatically perfect sentence: 'The ice-cream was boiling hot.' Grammatically fine — but something is deeply wrong. Time to go beyond grammar and dig into actual meaning.","teach":[{"title":"Semantic Analysis: Does It Make Sense?","body":"The third stage, semantic analysis, checks the literal, dictionary meaning of the words and phrases to see if the sentence actually makes sense. 'The ice-cream was boiling hot' is grammatically correct but semantically odd, since ice-cream is normally cold.","visual":"🧠"},{"title":"Logical Analysis: The Bigger Picture","body":"The final stage, logical analysis, looks at the text as a whole, using real-world knowledge and the context of earlier sentences to draw out its complete, intended meaning — including figuring out what pronouns like 'it' or 'she' refer to.","visual":"🔎"},{"title":"All Four Stages Work Together","body":"A machine understands human language by moving through all four stages in order: lexical (words), then syntax (grammar), then semantic (literal meaning), then logical (full, contextual meaning). Skipping any stage can lead to a misunderstanding.","visual":"🧩"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which NLP stage would flag 'The ice-cream was boiling hot' as odd, even though its grammar is correct?","options":["Semantic analysis, because the meaning doesn't make sense","Lexical analysis, because 'ice-cream' is not a word","Syntax analysis, because the word order is wrong","None of the stages would catch this"],"answer":0,"explain":"Semantic analysis checks whether the meaning of the words together actually makes sense."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the sentences 'Riya finished her project. She was proud of it,' figuring out that 'she' means Riya and 'it' means the project happens in which stage?","options":["Logical analysis","Lexical analysis","Syntax analysis","Keyword extraction"],"answer":0,"explain":"Logical analysis looks at the wider context to resolve references and build the complete meaning."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the correct order of the four stages of NLP?","options":["Lexical → Syntax → Semantic → Logical","Logical → Semantic → Syntax → Lexical","Syntax → Lexical → Logical → Semantic","Semantic → Logical → Lexical → Syntax"],"answer":0,"explain":"NLP moves from individual words, to grammar, to literal meaning, to full contextual meaning."}]},{"id":"nl6","title":"Script Bots vs Smart Bots","icon":"🗨","kind":"lesson","story":"Two chatbot windows pop open side by side. One only replies with a handful of canned answers no matter what you type. The other seems to actually get what you mean and remembers what you said earlier. Time to tell them apart.","teach":[{"title":"What Is a Chatbot?","body":"A chatbot is a computer program designed to simulate a conversation with a human user, usually through typed or spoken messages, to answer questions or help with tasks.","visual":"🗨"},{"title":"Script Bots: Simple and Rule-Based","body":"A script bot works on pre-defined rules and scripts written by a programmer. It can only respond to a limited set of exact questions or keywords, doesn't really understand language, and can't handle anything outside its script — an early example is ELIZA, a 1966 chatbot that matched patterns in typed sentences.","visual":"📋"},{"title":"Smart Bots: Powered by NLP","body":"A smart bot uses NLP and machine learning to understand the actual meaning behind what you type or say, so it can handle a huge variety of phrasing, learn from conversations, and hold more natural exchanges. Google Assistant, Alexa and Siri are all smart bots.","visual":"🧠"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the key difference between a script bot and a smart bot?","options":["A script bot follows fixed pre-written rules, while a smart bot uses NLP to understand language flexibly","A script bot is always more advanced than a smart bot","A script bot uses machine learning, a smart bot does not","There is no real difference between them"],"answer":0,"explain":"Script bots are rule-based and limited; smart bots use NLP and machine learning to understand varied, natural input."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is a classic example of a script bot, known for its simple, pattern-matching replies?","options":["ELIZA","Google Assistant","Alexa","Siri"],"answer":0,"explain":"ELIZA (1966) matched patterns in text using scripted rules, without truly understanding language."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why can a smart bot like Google Assistant handle many different ways of asking the same question, while a script bot cannot?","options":["Because it uses NLP to understand meaning, not just match exact pre-set phrases","Because it has a much larger screen","Because it only works with typed text, never speech","Because it was programmed before script bots were invented"],"answer":0,"explain":"NLP lets smart bots interpret varied phrasing and context instead of relying on fixed scripts."}]},{"id":"nl7","title":"Cleaning Up Text: Normalisation","icon":"🧹","kind":"lesson","story":"Before the Atlas AI can analyse a paragraph, it needs to tidy it up first — lowercase the letters, split it into words, and trim away the clutter. Welcome to text normalisation.","teach":[{"title":"Tokenization: Splitting Into Pieces","body":"Tokenization breaks a paragraph into sentences, and each sentence into individual words called tokens. This gives the computer small, manageable pieces of text to work with instead of one long block.","visual":"✂"},{"title":"Lowercasing and Removing Clutter","body":"Text is usually converted to lowercase so that 'Cat' and 'cat' are treated as the same word. Punctuation, special characters and common stopwords — tiny filler words like 'the', 'is' and 'and' that carry little meaning on their own — are often removed too.","visual":"🧹"},{"title":"Stemming and Lemmatization","body":"Stemming chops words down to a rough root form by simply cutting off suffixes, so 'studies' might become 'studi' — fast, but not always a real word. Lemmatization is more careful: it reduces a word to its proper dictionary base form, called a lemma, so 'studies' correctly becomes 'study'.","visual":"🌱"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does tokenization do to a piece of text?","options":["Breaks it into sentences and then individual words (tokens)","Translates it into another language","Deletes all the vowels","Converts it into a picture"],"answer":0,"explain":"Tokenization splits text into smaller units — sentences and then words — for further processing."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why are stopwords like 'the', 'is' and 'and' often removed during text normalisation?","options":["They appear very frequently but carry little meaning on their own","They are always spelled incorrectly","They only appear in questions, never statements","They make the text file too small"],"answer":0,"explain":"Stopwords are common filler words that add little to the specific meaning of a text, so removing them highlights the more meaningful words."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the key difference between stemming and lemmatization?","options":["Stemming roughly chops off endings; lemmatization finds the proper dictionary base word","Stemming only works on numbers; lemmatization only works on letters","Stemming adds new words; lemmatization deletes words","There is no real difference"],"answer":0,"explain":"Stemming is a quick, rough cut of suffixes, while lemmatization returns an actual valid dictionary form."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is the correct general order for basic text normalisation?","options":["Tokenize the text, then lowercase and clean it, then stem or lemmatize the words","Stem the words, then invent new sentences, then delete the file","Translate to French, then tokenize, then capitalize everything","Remove all letters, then add punctuation back"],"answer":0,"explain":"A typical pipeline first splits text into tokens, then cleans and lowercases it, then reduces words to root forms."}]},{"id":"nl8","title":"Counting Words: Bag-of-Words and TF-IDF","icon":"📊","kind":"lesson","story":"The Atlas AI wants to compare hundreds of book reviews at once — but it can't 'read' the way you do. Instead, it turns every review into a simple table of word counts. Let's see how.","teach":[{"title":"Bag-of-Words: Just Count the Words","body":"The Bag-of-Words (BoW) model represents a piece of text as a simple collection of its words and how many times each one appears — completely ignoring grammar and word order, like tipping all the words out of a sentence into a bag.","visual":"🛍"},{"title":"Building a Bag-of-Words Table","body":"To build a BoW model: normalise the text, list every unique word to form a vocabulary, then count how many times each vocabulary word appears in each document. The result is a simple table of numbers a computer can easily compare.","visual":"🔢"},{"title":"TF-IDF: Which Words Really Matter?","body":"TF-IDF, short for Term Frequency - Inverse Document Frequency, improves on plain word counting. Term Frequency (TF) measures how often a word appears in one document, while Inverse Document Frequency (IDF) lowers the score of words that appear in almost every document, like 'the' or 'is', and boosts words that are rare and distinctive across the whole collection.","visual":"⭐"},{"title":"Why TF-IDF Is Useful","body":"A high TF-IDF score means a word appears often in one particular document but rarely elsewhere — making it a strong clue to what that document is specially about, unlike common words that appear everywhere and reveal little.","visual":"🎯"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the sentence 'the cat sat on the mat and the cat purred', how many times does the word 'the' appear?","answer":3,"explain":"'the' appears in 'the cat', 'on the mat' and 'the cat purred' — three times."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using Bag-of-Words on the sentence 'AI is fun and NLP is fun too', how many times does the word 'fun' appear?","answer":2,"explain":"'fun' appears twice: 'AI is fun' and 'NLP is fun too'."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is a major limitation of the plain Bag-of-Words model?","options":["It ignores word order and grammar, treating text as just a collection of words","It cannot count words at all","It only works on numbers, not text","It requires no normalisation of text"],"answer":0,"explain":"BoW throws away sentence structure and order, keeping only word counts."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In TF-IDF, what does a high score for a word in a document usually mean?","options":["That word is frequent in this document but rare across other documents, making it distinctive","That word is a stopword like 'the' or 'is'","The document is very short","The word does not appear in the document at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"TF-IDF rewards words that are common in one document but uncommon overall — a sign of what makes that document distinct."}]},{"id":"nl-boss","title":"Final Boss: ECHO-9, the Glitchy Chatbot","icon":"👾","kind":"boss","story":"The Atlas server room flickers red. ECHO-9, an old chatbot patched together from every lesson you've learned, blocks the exit with garbled text scrolling across its screen. Only a full grasp of NLP will get you past it.","teach":[{"title":"Everything Comes Together","body":"You've learned why language is hard for machines, what NLP is, where it's used, its four stages, chatbot types, and how text is turned into numbers with Bag-of-Words and TF-IDF. Time to prove it.","visual":"💬"}],"boss":{"name":"ECHO-9, the Glitchy Chatbot","taunt":"Beep-bloop! I've swallowed a billion sentences and I still don't get your jokes — prove you understand human language better than I do!"},"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is human language especially hard for computers to process?","options":["It's ambiguous, depends on context, and keeps changing over time","It only has 10 words total","It has no grammar rules at all","Computers cannot store letters"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ambiguity, context-dependence and a constantly evolving vocabulary all make language hard for machines."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"NLP is best described as…","options":["A branch of AI that lets computers understand and generate human language","A type of computer chip","A method for compressing images","A way to speed up the internet"],"answer":0,"explain":"NLP is the AI field focused on human language understanding and generation."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which NLP application is at work when a customer review is automatically labelled 'positive' or 'negative'?","options":["Sentiment analysis","Keyword extraction","Tokenization","Stemming"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sentiment analysis detects the opinion or emotion expressed in text."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Put the four stages of NLP in the correct order.","options":["Lexical, Syntax, Semantic, Logical","Syntax, Lexical, Logical, Semantic","Logical, Semantic, Syntax, Lexical","Semantic, Lexical, Syntax, Logical"],"answer":0,"explain":"NLP moves from words, to grammar, to literal meaning, to full contextual meaning."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A script bot and a smart bot differ mainly because…","options":["A script bot follows fixed rules, a smart bot uses NLP to understand varied language","A script bot is powered by NLP, a smart bot is not","Both are exactly the same technology","A smart bot cannot hold any conversation"],"answer":0,"explain":"Script bots rely on pre-written rules; smart bots use NLP and machine learning to understand flexible input."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Removing filler words like 'the' and 'is', and reducing 'running' to 'run', are both part of…","options":["Text normalisation","Sentiment analysis","Voice recognition hardware","Keyword hacking"],"answer":0,"explain":"Removing stopwords and reducing words to root forms (stemming/lemmatization) are steps of text normalisation."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using Bag-of-Words, how many times does the word 'is' appear in: 'NLP is powerful and NLP is fun'?","answer":2,"explain":"'is' appears twice: 'NLP is powerful' and 'NLP is fun'."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does TF-IDF help identify in a document?","options":["Words that are frequent in that document but rare elsewhere, showing what it's specially about","The total number of documents in a library","The exact translation of a sentence","The grammatical subject of a sentence"],"answer":0,"explain":"TF-IDF highlights words that are distinctive to a document rather than common everywhere."}]}]},{"id":"advancepython10","index":6,"name":"Advance Python","subtitle":"Artificial Intelligence — Unit 7","emoji":"🐍","palette":{"from":"#c084fc","to":"#581c87"},"lessons":[{"id":"ap1","title":"The Notebook of Whispering Cells","icon":"📓","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in the Data Spire, you find an ancient tool that lets scholars run tiny spells one piece at a time and see the result instantly. To pass, you must learn to read its cells: the Jupyter Notebook.","teach":[{"title":"What is Jupyter Notebook?","body":"Jupyter Notebook is an interactive coding environment that runs in your browser. Instead of running a whole script at once, you write and run small chunks called cells, seeing each result immediately below it.","visual":"📓"},{"title":"Code cells vs Markdown cells","body":"A code cell holds Python you run with Shift+Enter; a Markdown cell holds plain text, headings, or notes explaining what the code does. Mixing both makes notebooks great for showing your work step by step.","visual":"📝"},{"title":"Output appears inline","body":"When a cell prints a value, shows a table, or draws a chart, the result appears right below that cell, not in a separate window. This makes it easy to test one small idea at a time on real data.","visual":"👀"},{"title":"The kernel remembers","body":"Behind the notebook runs a kernel, a live Python process that remembers every variable you have created so far. Running cells out of order can get confusing, so it usually helps to run them top to bottom.","visual":"⚙️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is a 'cell' in a Jupyter Notebook?","options":["A small block of code or text you can run and see the result of separately","A type of Python data structure","A folder that stores your files","A shortcut key for saving"],"answer":0,"explain":"Notebooks are built from cells, you run them one at a time and see the output right there."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which keyboard shortcut commonly runs the currently selected cell in Jupyter Notebook?","options":["Shift+Enter","Ctrl+Z","Alt+F4","Tab"],"answer":0,"explain":"Shift+Enter runs the current cell and moves to the next one."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the 'kernel' in a Jupyter Notebook?","options":["The running Python process that keeps track of your variables as you execute cells","A special type of chart","The name of the notebook file","A built-in Python library"],"answer":0,"explain":"The kernel is the live process executing your code and holding onto your variables between cells."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why do data scientists like using notebooks to explore data?","options":["They can test small pieces of code and immediately see tables or charts as output","Notebooks run code faster than any other tool","Notebooks don't allow any text or notes","Notebooks require no installation ever"],"answer":0,"explain":"Seeing results immediately after each small step makes exploring data much easier."}]},{"id":"ap2","title":"Guild of a Thousand Toolkits","icon":"📦","kind":"lesson","story":"The path forward is blocked by a locked toolbox. A guild keeper explains: powerful tools already exist, you just need to fetch the right ones with the right key, and keep your toolbox tidy for each quest.","teach":[{"title":"What is a package or library?","body":"A Python package (or library) is reusable code someone else already wrote and tested, like NumPy for math, pandas for tables, or matplotlib for charts. Instead of writing everything from scratch, you import a library and use its ready-made tools.","visual":"📦"},{"title":"Installing with pip","body":"pip is Python's package installer. Typing pip install numpy in a terminal downloads that library from PyPI (the Python Package Index) and makes it available to import in your programs.","visual":"⬇️"},{"title":"Importing a library","body":"Once installed, you bring a library into your program with an import statement, often with a short nickname, for example import numpy as np or import pandas as pd. Then you use its tools, like np.mean() or pd.read_csv().","visual":"🔌"},{"title":"Why virtual environments?","body":"A virtual environment is a separate, isolated Python setup for one project, with its own copy of installed packages. This way one project can use an older library version while another uses a newer one, without them clashing.","visual":"🧰"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is a Python package (library) used for?","options":["Providing ready-made, reusable code so you don't have to write everything from scratch","Making your computer run faster","Replacing the need to write any Python code at all","Automatically fixing bugs in your program"],"answer":0,"explain":"Libraries package up tested code, like math functions or chart tools, for anyone to reuse."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which command installs a package named pandas using pip?","options":["pip install pandas","import pandas","python pandas","get pandas"],"answer":0,"explain":"pip install <package name> downloads and installs a library from PyPI."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"After installing a library, how do you bring it into your Python program?","options":["With an import statement, e.g. import numpy as np","By restarting your computer","By pasting its code manually every time","Libraries load automatically with no code needed"],"answer":0,"explain":"import makes the library's functions available to use in your code."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why might a programmer use a virtual environment for a project?","options":["To keep that project's installed packages separate from other projects, avoiding version conflicts","To make the internet connection faster","To delete unused Python files automatically","To write code without using Python syntax"],"answer":0,"explain":"Virtual environments isolate each project's packages so different projects don't interfere with each other."}]},{"id":"ap3","title":"Arrays of the Swift Current","icon":"🔢","kind":"lesson","story":"Ahead, a river of numbers flows too fast for ordinary lists to cross. Only NumPy's arrays can move through it at speed, treating a whole column of numbers as one single unit.","teach":[{"title":"Meet NumPy","body":"NumPy (Numerical Python) is a library built for fast, whole-array math on numbers. You typically load it with import numpy as np, then use functions like np.array(), np.mean(), and np.median().","visual":"🔢"},{"title":"Making an array","body":"np.array() turns a normal Python list into a NumPy array, for example marks = np.array([10, 20, 20, 30, 40]). Arrays look like lists but support fast, element-by-element math.","visual":"🧮"},{"title":"Lists add by joining, not adding","body":"Careful: with plain Python lists, [1, 2] + [3, 4] gives [1, 2, 3, 4]. It joins (concatenates) the lists, it does not add the numbers together. To add two lists of numbers element by element, you loop through them or convert to NumPy arrays first.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"NumPy adds element-wise","body":"With NumPy arrays, the same + behaves differently: np.array([1, 2]) + np.array([3, 4]) gives array([4, 6]). Each position is added to the matching position in the other array.","visual":"✨"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In plain Python, what does [1, 2, 3] + [4, 5, 6] produce?","options":["[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]","[5, 7, 9]","[1, 2, 3]","An error"],"answer":0,"explain":"The + operator on plain Python lists joins (concatenates) them, it does not add numbers together."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does np.array([1, 2]) + np.array([3, 4]) produce?","options":["array([4, 6])","array([1, 2, 3, 4])","array([3, 4, 1, 2])","An error, arrays can't be added"],"answer":0,"explain":"NumPy arrays add element-wise: 1+3=4 and 2+4=6."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which line correctly turns a Python list into a NumPy array?","options":["np.array([10, 20, 30])","np.list([10, 20, 30])","numpy.make([10, 20, 30])","array.np([10, 20, 30])"],"answer":0,"explain":"np.array() is the standard way to build a NumPy array from a Python list."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why do data programs often convert lists into NumPy arrays?","options":["Arrays support fast, element-wise math operations that plain lists don't do directly","Arrays can never hold decimal numbers","Lists are not allowed in Python anymore","Arrays automatically sort themselves"],"answer":0,"explain":"NumPy arrays are built for efficient numeric operations across every element at once."}]},{"id":"ap4","title":"Oracle of Middle Numbers","icon":"📊","kind":"lesson","story":"An oracle guards the bridge, demanding you summarize a scroll of numbers in three ways before she reveals the path onward: its average, its middle, and its most common value.","teach":[{"title":"Mean, the average","body":"np.mean(data) adds up all the numbers and divides by how many there are. For scores = [10, 20, 20, 30, 40], np.mean(scores) gives 24.0.","visual":"➗"},{"title":"Median, the middle value","body":"np.median(data) sorts the numbers and picks the middle one (or averages the two middle ones if there's an even count). For the same scores [10, 20, 20, 30, 40], np.median(scores) gives 20.","visual":"🎯"},{"title":"Mode, the most frequent value","body":"The mode is whichever value appears most often. NumPy itself doesn't include a mode function, so Python's own statistics module is often used instead: statistics.mode(scores) returns 20, since it repeats twice.","visual":"🔁"},{"title":"Why all three matter","body":"Mean, median, and mode all describe a 'typical' value, but they can disagree. A few extreme scores can pull the mean up or down while the median stays steady, so comparing all three gives a fuller picture of the data.","visual":"⚖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For scores = [10, 20, 20, 30, 40], what is np.mean(scores)?","options":["24.0","20","30","120"],"answer":0,"explain":"(10+20+20+30+40) = 120, divided by 5 values = 24.0."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For scores = [10, 20, 20, 30, 40], what is np.median(scores)?","options":["20","24.0","30","25"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sorted, the values are 10, 20, 20, 30, 40; the middle (3rd) value is 20."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For scores = [10, 20, 20, 30, 40], what is the mode?","options":["20, because it appears most often (twice)","40, because it's the largest","24.0, same as the mean","10, because it's first in the list"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mode is the most frequently occurring value, and 20 appears twice, more than any other."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which NumPy functions directly calculate mean and median?","options":["np.mean() and np.median()","np.average_middle() and np.center()","np.avg() and np.mid()","np.total() and np.half()"],"answer":0,"explain":"np.mean() and np.median() are the standard NumPy functions for these two statistics."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why isn't there a np.mode() function used the same simple way as np.mean()?","options":["NumPy doesn't include a built-in mode function, so Python's statistics module (or SciPy) is used instead","Mode cannot be calculated for numbers, only for text","NumPy refuses to calculate mode for lists longer than 5 items","Mode is exactly the same as the median, so it isn't needed"],"answer":0,"explain":"Unlike mean and median, mode isn't built into NumPy directly; statistics.mode() is a common way to get it."}]},{"id":"ap5","title":"The Twin Chart Gates","icon":"📈","kind":"lesson","story":"Two stone gates bar the way, each demanding a different kind of chart. One wants a smooth line showing a trend. The other wants scattered dots showing how two things relate.","teach":[{"title":"matplotlib draws the charts","body":"matplotlib is Python's most common charting library. You usually load it with import matplotlib.pyplot as plt, build your chart, then call plt.show() to display it.","visual":"📈"},{"title":"Line chart, plt.plot()","body":"plt.plot(x, y) connects points with a line, in the order given. For example, plt.plot([2, 9], [5, 10]) draws a straight line from point (2,5) to point (9,10), great for showing a trend, like a change over time.","visual":"📉"},{"title":"Scatter chart, plt.scatter()","body":"plt.scatter(x, y) plots separate dots without connecting them. For points (2,5), (9,10), (8,3), (5,7), (6,18), you'd write plt.scatter([2,9,8,5,6], [5,10,3,7,18]), great for showing spread or relationship, with no order implied.","visual":"🔵"},{"title":"Choosing the right chart","body":"Use a line chart when the x-values follow a meaningful sequence (like days or steps) and you want to show a trend. Use a scatter chart when you just want to see how two variables relate, without suggesting one flows into the next.","visual":"🧭"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which matplotlib function draws a line chart connecting points in order?","options":["plt.plot()","plt.scatter()","plt.bar()","plt.line_only()"],"answer":0,"explain":"plt.plot() connects the given points with a line, in the order they're given."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which matplotlib function draws separate dots without connecting them?","options":["plt.scatter()","plt.plot()","plt.connect()","plt.dots_only()"],"answer":0,"explain":"plt.scatter() places individual points without joining them with lines."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"To draw a line from point (2,5) to point (9,10), which is correct?","options":["plt.plot([2, 9], [5, 10])","plt.plot([2, 5], [9, 10])","plt.scatter([2, 9])","plt.plot(2, 5, 9, 10)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The x-values [2, 9] and y-values [5, 10] are given as two separate lists, matching each point."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"You want to see whether hours studied and marks scored are related, without implying a trend line. Which chart fits best?","options":["A scatter chart","A line chart","No chart is needed","A markdown cell"],"answer":0,"explain":"A scatter chart shows the relationship between two variables as individual points, without connecting them in sequence."}]},{"id":"ap6","title":"The Archive of a Thousand Rows","icon":"🗂️","kind":"lesson","story":"In the great Archive, data isn't stored as single numbers but as entire tables, rows and columns, just like a spreadsheet. pandas is the key that unlocks it.","teach":[{"title":"pandas and the DataFrame","body":"pandas is Python's library for working with tables of data. You load it with import pandas as pd, and its main object is the DataFrame, rows and columns, just like a spreadsheet, but inside your program.","visual":"🗂️"},{"title":"Reading a CSV file","body":"pd.read_csv('data.csv') reads a CSV (comma-separated values) file from your system and loads it into a DataFrame, for example df = pd.read_csv('marks.csv').","visual":"📥"},{"title":"Peeking with head()","body":"df.head() shows the first 5 rows of the DataFrame by default, a quick peek without printing the whole table. Pass a number to see more, e.g. df.head(10) shows the first 10 rows.","visual":"👁️"},{"title":"Summarizing with info()","body":"df.info() prints a summary: how many rows and columns exist, each column's name and data type (like int64 or object for text), and how many non-null (non-empty) values each column has.","visual":"📋"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which line reads a CSV file called marks.csv into a pandas DataFrame?","options":["df = pd.read_csv('marks.csv')","df = pd.open('marks.csv')","df = read.csv('marks.csv')","df = pd.load_csv('marks.csv')"],"answer":0,"explain":"pd.read_csv() is the standard pandas function for reading a CSV file into a DataFrame."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does df.head(10) do?","options":["Displays the first 10 rows of the DataFrame","Deletes the first 10 rows","Displays only column names","Displays the last 10 rows"],"answer":0,"explain":"head(n) shows the first n rows; head(10) shows the first 10."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What kind of information does df.info() give you?","options":["The number of rows/columns, each column's data type, and how many non-null values it has","Only the average of every column","A chart of the data","The exact pixel colors of an image"],"answer":0,"explain":"info() is a structural summary of the DataFrame: its shape, column types, and missing-value counts."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"By default, if you call df.head() with no number, how many rows does it show?","options":["5","1","10","All of them"],"answer":0,"explain":"head() defaults to showing the first 5 rows unless you pass a different number."}]},{"id":"ap7","title":"Vault of the Pixel Giants","icon":"🖼️","kind":"lesson","story":"The final vault stores not numbers or tables, but images, and to a computer, every image is secretly just a giant grid of numbers. Prove you can read one and measure its shape.","teach":[{"title":"An image is a grid of numbers","body":"To Python, a picture is just a NumPy array of pixel values. A library like matplotlib can read an image file straight into this array, e.g. img = plt.imread('photo.jpg'), the same plt you already used for charts.","visual":"🖼️"},{"title":"Displaying it","body":"Once loaded, plt.imshow(img) followed by plt.show() displays the image, just like plt.plot() displays a chart. matplotlib treats an image as another kind of picture to draw.","visual":"🖥️"},{"title":"Checking its shape","body":"img.shape returns a tuple like (height, width, channels). Height and width are measured in pixels, and channels is usually 3 for a color photo (Red, Green, Blue values at every pixel).","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Why shape matters for AI","body":"This is the same idea from the Computer Vision unit: models don't 'see' an image, they process its array of numbers. Knowing an image's shape, its height, width, and channels, is the first check before feeding it into any vision model.","visual":"🔍"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In Python, what actually IS an image once you've read it with a library like matplotlib?","options":["A NumPy array of pixel number values","A single text string","A sound wave","A type of CSV file"],"answer":0,"explain":"Images are read in as arrays of numbers representing pixel values, that's what lets Python and AI models work with them."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does img.shape typically return for a color photo?","options":["A tuple like (height, width, channels)","A single number, the file size","The image's filename","The average brightness only"],"answer":0,"explain":"shape gives (height, width, channels), the picture's dimensions and how many color channels each pixel has."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For a standard color (RGB) photo, how many channels does img.shape usually show?","options":["3","1","10","0"],"answer":0,"explain":"Color images usually have 3 channels, Red, Green, and Blue values for every pixel."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does checking an image's shape matter before using it with a Computer Vision model?","options":["Models process the image as an array of numbers, so knowing its dimensions is an important first check","Shape has no connection to Computer Vision at all","Only the filename matters to a model, not the shape","Checking shape deletes unnecessary pixels automatically"],"answer":0,"explain":"This connects directly to the Computer Vision unit: vision models work on the pixel array, so its shape (height, width, channels) matters."}]},{"id":"ap-boss","title":"The Grand Pipeline Convergence","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","story":"At the heart of the Study Atlas, every path you've walked converges: modeling, evaluation, vision, language, and now Python's own toolbox. A colossus built from everything you've mastered rises for one final trial.","teach":[{"title":"One pipeline, many tools","body":"A real AI project strings together everything you've learned: pandas to load and explore data, NumPy for quick statistics, matplotlib to visualize it, and, for vision or language projects, libraries to handle images or text before any model is trained.","visual":"🧩"},{"title":"From Class 10's journey to here","body":"You've defined problems and built models, evaluated how good those models were, taught computers to see with Computer Vision, and taught them to read with NLP. Advanced Python is the toolbox that makes all of that possible in real code.","visual":"🗺️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which tool would you open first to write and test small pieces of Python step by step, seeing results immediately?","options":["Jupyter Notebook","A CSV file","A virtual environment","matplotlib alone"],"answer":0,"explain":"Jupyter Notebook lets you run small cells and see results right away, perfect for exploring data step by step."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Before you can import a library like pandas that isn't built into Python, what must you do first?","options":["Install it, e.g. with pip install pandas","Restart Python entirely","Rewrite it yourself","Nothing, all libraries are pre-installed"],"answer":0,"explain":"Libraries outside Python's standard set must be installed first, commonly using pip."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"You have marks = [12, 18, 18, 24, 30, 42]. Using NumPy, what does np.mean(marks) give?","options":["24.0","18","30","144"],"answer":0,"explain":"(12+18+18+24+30+42) = 144, divided by 6 values = 24.0."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For the same marks = [12, 18, 18, 24, 30, 42], what is the mode?","options":["18, since it's the only value that repeats","42, since it's the largest","24.0, since that's the mean","12, since it's first"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mode is the most frequent value; 18 appears twice, more than any other value here."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"You want to show how a robot's error rate changed step by step during training. Which chart type fits best?","options":["A line chart, since it shows a trend across an ordered sequence","A scatter chart, since order doesn't matter","No chart, just read the raw numbers","A pie chart of individual errors"],"answer":0,"explain":"A line chart is ideal for showing trends over an ordered sequence, like training steps."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which pandas method quickly shows a dataset's column names, data types, and missing values, the kind of check you'd want before using that data to build a model?","options":["df.info()","df.plot()","df.mean()","df.imshow()"],"answer":0,"explain":"df.info() gives a structural summary of the data, a useful step before modeling on it."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the Computer Vision unit, models process images as arrays of numbers. Which line of code checks an image's height, width, and color channels?","options":["img.shape","img.head()","img.mean()","img.read_csv()"],"answer":0,"explain":"img.shape returns (height, width, channels), the same array-of-numbers idea from the Computer Vision unit."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Looking back at your whole Class 10 AI journey, modeling, evaluation, computer vision, and NLP, what best describes the role of libraries like NumPy, pandas, and matplotlib?","options":["They're the practical tools that let you load, calculate, and visualize real data behind every AI project you built","They replace the need to understand AI concepts entirely","They only work for computer vision projects, not other units","They are only used for writing text documents"],"answer":0,"explain":"Across every unit, these libraries were the real, practical toolkit turning AI concepts into working code."}],"boss":{"name":"Codex Prime, the Data Colossus","taunt":"You've trained models, seen through pixels, and understood words. Now prove you can wire the entire toolbox together in one final run!"}}]}];